====================================================================
DOMAIN: From Polis to Empire -- Sittlichkeit, Legal Personhood, and the Ancient/Modern Transition
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-07
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 6, Medium: 8, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CrossRef, Brave web search. OpenAlex and CORE were rate-limited (HTTP 429) throughout this session; OpenAlex returned two usable abstracts on retry, CORE returned none. Semantic Scholar, PhilPapers, CrossRef, and Brave web search were used as fallbacks throughout.

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Hegel's broader narrative -- spanning the Phenomenology of Spirit's Spirit chapter and the Lectures on the Philosophy of World History -- of how the immediate, "beautiful" ethical substance (Sittlichkeit) of the Greek polis collapses into the abstract legal personhood of the "condition of right" (Rechtszustand) under the Roman Empire, and how this in turn gives way to the "world of self-alienated spirit" (the world of culture, Bildung) that eventually issues in modern subjectivity, civil society, and the rational constitutional state. Two literatures converge here. The first is systematic commentary on the Philosophy of Right's non-historical triad of family, civil society, and state (Hardimon, Smith, Wolsing, Hristov, Bykova), which asks how the historical narrative of Sittlichkeit's collapse and reconstitution maps onto Hegel's dialectical (not chronological) derivation of ethical life's moments. The second is the historiographical and methodological literature on Hegel's philosophy of history as such (McCarney, Bernasconi, Tibebu, Baumann, Losurdo, Lima, Houlgate), which debates whether Hegel's teleological narrative -- Oriental world, Greek world, Roman world, Germanic/Christian world -- is a defensible philosophical reconstruction of the development of freedom or an ideologically Eurocentric, racially inflected, or authoritarian imposition of necessity on contingent history. A live exchange between Bernasconi and McCarney over the "racial basis" of Hegel's Eurocentrism exemplifies this second literature at its sharpest, and recent work (Lima 2024, Baumann 2020) continues to test whether Hegel's own conceptual resources can answer or must simply concede these charges. Daniel Loick and Chad Kautzer's close reading of the short "Condition of Right" chapter of the Phenomenology provides the most direct textual engagement with the specific Roman-legal-personhood transition named in the domain brief, while Hristov's study of the Roman republican background to Hegel's constitutional theory shows that the "Roman" moment does not simply disappear once superseded but leaves a structural residue in Hegel's account of the modern state.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
The overall project examines Hegel's Antigone and his treatment of Greek Sittlichkeit; this domain supplies the "far side" of that story -- what Hegel thinks happens after Antigone's ethical world collapses, and how that collapse (via Rome and Bildung) is retrospectively organized, in the systematic Philosophy of Right, into a triad in which family, civil society, and the state recapitulate and transform, rather than simply abandon, the ethical immediacy dramatized in Antigone's world. It thus grounds any claim the project makes about whether Hegel's Antigone reading is offered as nostalgia for a lost ethical wholeness or as a diagnosis of a necessary and superseded stage.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Coverage of Duncan Forbes is limited to his edited/introduced translation of the Lectures on the Philosophy of World History rather than an independent secondary-literature contribution, since no standalone Forbes article or monograph on this transition surfaced in search; it is omitted here as primarily a primary-text edition rather than commentary. No dedicated recent (2024--2026) monograph-length treatment of the Roman "legal status" chapter specifically (as opposed to the philosophy of history generally) was located beyond Loick and Kautzer's article and Hristov's article; this remains a comparatively thin sub-literature relative to the Eurocentrism debate. Eight entries (Hardimon, Smith, McCarney's guidebook, McCarney's 2003 exchange, Losurdo, Loick and Kautzer, Tibebu, Bykova) could not be matched with an authentic abstract from Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, or CORE during automated enrichment -- for five of these, the enrichment script's NDPR fallback initially returned abstracts from unrelated book reviews (a mismatch caught and removed during quality review) -- and they are marked INCOMPLETE accordingly. Their CORE ARGUMENT notes are instead grounded in publisher descriptions, contemporary scholarly book reviews, and (for articles) content retrieved via targeted web search, as documented during research; no bibliographic field in any entry was filled from unverified memory.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Consider organizing this domain's material along the two axes identified above: (1) the systematic/institutional literature (Hardimon, Smith, Wolsing, Hristov, Bykova) explaining how the family/civil-society/state triad recapitulates and transforms Greek Sittlichkeit's immediacy, and (2) the historiographical/methodological literature (McCarney, Bernasconi, Tibebu, Baumann, Losurdo, Lima, Houlgate) debating the legitimacy of the teleological narrative that carries Sittlichkeit from Greece through Rome to modernity. The Bernasconi/McCarney exchange and Loick and Kautzer's close reading of the "Condition of Right" chapter are natural anchor texts for, respectively, the methodological and the specifically Roman-legal-personhood strands.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Systematic reconciliation readings (Hardimon, Smith, Wolsing): modern ethical life preserves but transforms Greek Sittlichkeit's substantiality by adding reflective, institutionally mediated freedom.
- Ambivalent/critical readings of modernity (Houlgate, Hristov, Loick and Kautzer): modern legal and constitutional freedom does not simply supersede but partly reproduces or fails fully to resolve the abstraction and fragility first diagnosed in the Roman condition of right.
- Methodological critique of the philosophy-of-history narrative (Bernasconi, Tibebu): Hegel's Greek-Rome-Germanic teleology is structurally Eurocentric and racially inflected, not merely incidentally biased.
- Defenses against the authoritarian/Eurocentric charge (McCarney, Baumann, Losurdo, Lima): Hegel's philosophy of history and modern state can be read as defensible, non-authoritarian, and even partially self-critical once properly reconstructed.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Critical and Classicist Responses — Historical, Philological, and Philosophical Critiques
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-07
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 6, Medium: 7, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef, Brave web search. CORE was persistently rate-limited (HTTP 429) throughout this session and returned no usable results for this domain; OpenAlex was rate-limited during the search phase but worked during abstract enrichment. Semantic Scholar, PhilPapers, CrossRef, OpenAlex, and Brave web search served as the working sources.

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain gathers material that tests Hegel's reading of Antigone and Sittlichkeit against two independent bodies of scholarship: classical philology/anthropology of Greek religion, law, and gender, and philosophical work on tragic agency, moral luck, and practical necessity that does not originate within Hegel scholarship itself. Two clusters dominate. The first is the francophone structuralist/anthropological tradition inaugurated by Jean-Pierre Vernant and Pierre Vidal-Naquet, who argue that fifth-century tragedy stages an unresolved tension between an emergent, still-fragile legal/political vocabulary and older religious, mythic, and kinship-based modes of thought — a reading that implicitly displaces Hegel's confident dialectical resolution (family/state, divine law/human law) with genuine, historically located ambiguity. Christiane Sourvinou-Inwood's methodological critique of "readings" of the Antigone that import modern (including idealist) assumptions about intention, gender, and law onto a text whose original audience shared none of them belongs to the same current, as does Oudemans and Lardinois's book-length argument that the play resists any philosophical schematization — Hegel's included — because its "tragic ambiguity" is irreducible to conceptual synthesis. The second cluster is Anglophone moral philosophy's engagement with Greek tragic ethics via Bernard Williams's Shame and Necessity, which directly challenges the progressivist narrative (shared by Hegel) that moves from an archaic "shame culture" governed by necessity to a modern culture of reflective autonomy and guilt; Williams argues the Greeks already possessed something like our own moral psychology, undercutting the developmental story Hegel's Phenomenology depends on. Martha Nussbaum's Fragility of Goodness reads the Antigone as staging genuine moral luck and practical conflict rather than a clean opposition of ethical substances, implicitly resisting Hegel's account of a "necessary" tragic guilt that both sides deserve. Reviews and critical notices of Williams (Irwin, Gay) and recent work extending the Tat/Handlung debate (Han-Pile) and Williams's legacy (Russell) sharpen the philosophical stakes of "necessity" and responsibility that Hegel's reading of Antigone and Oedipus presupposes. Classicist studies independent of Hegel entirely — Segal's Tragedy and Civilization, Foley's Female Acts in Greek Tragedy, Griffith's Cambridge commentary, and Loraux's Tragic Ways of Killing a Woman — supply the historically grounded picture of Sophoclean drama, gender, heroism, and death against which Hegel's philosophical appropriation can be measured for adequacy.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Because the review's other domains largely work within the horizon of Hegel interpretation (textual exegesis, feminist reception, tragedy theory, comparative philosophy), this domain is essential to keeping the review genuinely critical: it supplies independent classicist and philosophical benchmarks against which Hegel's divine-law/human-law schema, his treatment of kinship and gender, and his account of tragic "necessity" and guilt can be assessed for historical and conceptual adequacy rather than merely elaborated.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Several items surfaced only as reviews of major works (e.g., a Japanese-language review of Griffith's commentary, an American Journal of Philology review of Loraux's book) rather than as independently indexed records of the primary books themselves; where this occurred, the primary book's bibliographic details were reconstructed from the review's own citation of it (title, translator, publisher, pagination) rather than fabricated, and DOIs were omitted where none exists at the book level. No classicist source was found that engages Hegel's Antigone reading by name and rejects it on narrowly art-historical or epigraphic grounds (e.g., funerary law, women's legal status in 5th-century Athens); the classicist critiques gathered here are methodological/interpretive rather than documentary-legal. Christopher Gill's and Nick Fisher's contemporaneous classicist reviews of Shame and Necessity were located via PhilPapers but could not be verified with sufficient bibliographic confidence (CrossRef match scores below threshold) and were excluded rather than included with uncertain metadata. Six entries (Vernant and Vidal-Naquet 1988, Gay 1989, Oudemans and Lardinois 1987, Segal 1981, Griffith 1999, Loraux 1987) are marked INCOMPLETE: no genuine abstract could be verified for them. For Vernant/Vidal-Naquet and Segal, the automated NDPR fallback initially attached abstracts from unrelated books (a review of an edited volume on Plato/Aristotle and equality, and a review of a book on Aristotle's Poetics, respectively) due to loose title matching; these mismatches were identified and removed during quality review rather than kept. For Gay, the only text retrievable from OpenAlex was a publisher-page citation stub rather than a genuine abstract, so it too was removed and the entry marked INCOMPLETE. Their CORE ARGUMENT notes are instead grounded in the works' established scholarly content as reflected in review titles, tables of contents, and bibliographic descriptions retrieved via the search scripts, not in unverified memory of specific bibliographic fields.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Pair Vernant/Vidal-Naquet, Sourvinou-Inwood, and Oudemans/Lardinois as the core "historical ambiguity vs. dialectical resolution" critique; pair Williams, Nussbaum, Irwin, Gay, Russell, and Han-Pile as the "necessity, guilt, and moral luck" philosophical critique; use Segal, Foley, Griffith, and Loraux as independent classicist controls for assessing specific claims Hegel makes about gender, heroism, and the family/city relation in the play.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Historical/anthropological ambiguity against dialectical resolution: 3 papers (Vernant and Vidal-Naquet, Sourvinou-Inwood, Oudemans and Lardinois) - argue the play's conflict resists the clean conceptual opposition Hegel imposes on it.
- Moral luck / necessity critique of the shame-to-guilt progress narrative: 5 papers (Williams, Nussbaum, Irwin, Gay, Russell) - challenge the developmental story in which Hegel's dialectic is embedded.
- Independent classicist studies of Sophoclean tragedy, gender, and heroism: 4 papers (Segal, Foley, Griffith, Loraux) - supply historically grounded readings against which Hegel's claims can be tested.
- Recent philosophical re-engagement with Hegel's action theory (Tat/Handlung) via Sophocles: 1 paper (Han-Pile) - extends the "necessity of ethical guilt" debate into contemporary action theory.
====================================================================
}

@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Comparative Philosophical Readings of Antigone (Kierkegaard, Lacan, Zizek, Derrida, Steiner)
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-07
PAPERS_FOUND: 16 total (High: 6, Medium: 10, Low: 0; 8 marked INCOMPLETE for missing abstract)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CrossRef, OpenAlex (partial, rate-limited)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain maps the major non-Hegelian and post-Hegelian philosophical
appropriations of Sophocles' Antigone that explicitly engage, contest, or
reframe Hegel's reading in the Phenomenology of Spirit. Five distinct lines
of reception emerge. (1) Kierkegaard's early aesthetic essay "The Tragic in
Ancient Drama Reflected in the Tragic in Modern Drama" (in Either/Or I)
stages a "modern Antigone" who, unlike Sophocles' heroine, suffers from a
reflective, inherited guilt (knowledge of Oedipus's crime) that isolates her
from any Hegelian reconciliation via ethical substance; Stevanovic, Jerr,
and Sestigiani trace how this move both borrows from and quietly displaces
Hegel's account of tragic collision. (2) Lacan's Seminar VII (The Ethics of
Psychoanalysis) relocates Antigone from the terrain of ethical substance and
mutual recognition to the register of desire and the death drive: Antigone
becomes the figure who "does not cede on her desire" and occupies the zone
between the two deaths, a reading that Moyaert, Cha, and Sjoholm situate
against Hegel's dialectic of divine and human law. (3) Zizek repeatedly
returns to Hegel's Antigone through a Lacanian lens, alternately radicalizing
and revising both thinkers; Dews's early critique and Robertson's later
intervention (against Zizek's "diabolical" reading) show how contested this
Hegelian-Lacanian synthesis remains, while Swiffen's account of the
Zizek-Butler debate demonstrates the political stakes of competing
Lacanian appropriations of the play. (4) Derrida's Glas subjects Hegel's
treatment of the family, the sister-brother bond, and Antigone's "peaceful"
recognition to a deconstructive reading that exposes the exclusions
(of desire, of the feminine, of what "remains") on which Hegel's dialectic
depends; Chanter, Kramer, Charen, and Baptist unpack different facets of
this confrontation. (5) George Steiner's Antigones (1984) supplies the
reception-historical frame within which all these readings are situated,
arguing that the German idealists (and Hegel above all) made Antigone the
"pivot of consciousness" for Western thought; Ponton's review-essay assesses
Steiner's placement of Hegel against Aristotle in this genealogy.

Across all five lines, a common structural pattern recurs: each thinker
takes Hegel's dialectical resolution of the Antigone-Creon conflict (via
mutual recognition, ethical Bildung, or the passage from Sittlichkeit to
modern subjectivity) as the target against which a rival logic--of
irreducible reflective guilt (Kierkegaard), of desire beyond the good
(Lacan/Zizek), or of an undialectizable remainder (Derrida)--is
counterposed. This makes the domain less a set of isolated "receptions"
than a sustained argument about whether Hegel's reconciliation of Antigone's
particular ethical claim with the universal is achieved, repressed, or
merely displaced.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the post-Hegelian horizon against which the
dissertation's account of Hegel's Antigone and Sittlichkeit can be tested:
each thinker treated here reads Hegel's resolution of the Antigone-Creon
conflict as either incomplete (Kierkegaard), theoretically mistaken about
what actually drives Antigone (Lacan), or covertly violent toward what it
excludes (Derrida). Engaging this literature allows the project to show
that debates over Hegel's Antigone did not end with Hegel scholarship
proper but became a recurring test case for post-Hegelian philosophy's
self-definition against dialectical reconciliation.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Coverage of Kierkegaard's essay itself (as opposed to secondary literature
on it) and of Lacan's Seminar VII text itself is deliberately left to the
primary-text domain; this domain could not independently verify a DOI for
Dews's 1995 Radical Philosophy piece or for Sestigiani's Colloquy article
(both open-access/older venues without CrossRef records), and OpenAlex was
persistently rate-limited during this search, so abstract coverage relies
more heavily on Semantic Scholar/CrossRef/OpenAlex-via-CrossRef-DOI-lookup
than in other domains. Eight entries are marked INCOMPLETE for lacking a
verifiable abstract (mostly older articles, book chapters, and the two
books); note that the NDPR fallback initially attached an abstract from an
unrelated book (Robert Kane's The Complex Tapestry of Free Will) to
Sjoholm's The Antigone Complex via loose title matching--this mismatch was
caught and removed, and the entry marked INCOMPLETE instead. The stray SEP
context-extraction attempt (Stage 5.6) for the Kierkegaard/Lacan/Derrida
biographical SEP entries did not complete in reasonable time and was
abandoned, since these general biographical entries are unlikely to contain
Antigone-specific bibliography matches for the High-importance papers here.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize by the five reception-lines (Kierkegaard, Lacan, Zizek, Derrida,
Steiner) rather than chronologically, since the Zizek material is itself
explicitly mediated through Lacan and frequently responds to Hegel through
Lacan's prior displacement of him; flag Steiner's Antigones as the
framing/background text for the whole domain rather than as a peer
contribution to any one debate.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Reflective/modern guilt beyond ethical substance (Kierkegaard reception): 3 papers - Antigone's modern refiguration carries hereditary, subjective guilt that exceeds Hegel's category of ethical substance, producing isolation rather than reconciliation.
- Desire and the death drive against ethical conflict (Lacan reception): 3 papers - Antigone's act is relocated from the clash of divine/human law to an ethics of desire "beyond the good," reframing Hegel's Sittlichkeit as inadequate to what actually compels her.
- Hegelian-Lacanian dialectics and their contestation (Zizek and critics): 3 papers - Zizek's fusion of Hegel and Lacan is itself a live site of dispute over whether it illuminates or distorts both thinkers' accounts of Antigone.
- Deconstruction of the Hegelian family and the "remainder" (Derrida reception): 4 papers - Glas reads Hegel's treatment of the sister-brother bond and Antigone's recognition as structured by exclusions that dialectical Aufhebung cannot absorb.
- Reception-historical framing (Steiner): 2 papers - Positions Hegel's reading within a longer genealogy in which Antigone becomes philosophy's privileged self-reflective test case.
====================================================================
}

@misc{hegel1977phenomenology,
    author = "Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich",
    title = "Phenomenology of Spirit",
    year = "1977",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: The primary locus of Hegel's Antigone reading. Chapter VI.A ("The True Spirit: Ethical Life," corresponding to pp. 266-289 in this Miller translation) argues that the immediate, unreflective ethical substance (Sittlichkeit) of the Greek polis necessarily splits into two mutually exclusive and equally justified "laws": the divine law of the family (the unwritten law governing the dead and kinship, embodied by woman/Antigone) and the human law of the state (the law of the actual, self-conscious community, embodied by man/Creon). Antigone and Creon each act rightly according to one law while necessarily violating the other; the resulting tragic collision destroys both individuals and, for Hegel, marks the self-undoing of Greek ethical substance as such, propelling Spirit toward the world of legal personhood ("Legal Status," discussed at pp. 290ff., corresponding to the Roman world). RELEVANCE: This is the single most heavily cited primary passage in the secondary literature this review surveys; nearly every debate downstream (feminist reception, tragedy theory, polis-to-modernity narrative) takes this chapter as its point of departure. Translation and section numbering here (A.V. Miller, with paragraph numbers) is the standard English point of reference. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "primary-text, Phenomenology-of-Spirit, ethical-life, Antigone, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hegel1991elements,
    author = "Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich",
    editor = "Wood, Allen W.",
    title = "Elements of the Philosophy of Right",
    year = "1991",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: The mature systematic treatment of ethical life (Sittlichkeit, Part Three, §§142-360), dividing it into Family (§§158-181), Civil Society (§§182-256), and the State (§§257-360). The family section culminates at §166, whose Remark (Anmerkung) contains Hegel's controversial claim that "one sex is mind in its self-diremption into explicit personal self-subsistence...the other sex is mind maintaining itself in unity," associating masculinity with civil/political life and femininity with the family's "substantial" ethical disposition -- a passage read by many commentators as Hegel's systematic, non-narrative counterpart to the Phenomenology's Antigone. §166's Remark explicitly invokes Sophocles' Antigone as authority ("the ethical life which is most inherently divine...appears in the words of Sophocles' Antigone"). RELEVANCE: Supplies the systematic (as opposed to narrative-dialectical) statement of the gendered division of ethical labor that the Phenomenology dramatizes through Antigone, and is the required primary reference point for the feminist-reception and gender debates handled in other domains. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "primary-text, Philosophy-of-Right, Sittlichkeit, section-166, gender, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hegel1975aesthetics,
    author = "Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich",
    title = "Aesthetics: Lectures on Fine Art",
    year = "1975",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: In the discussion of dramatic poetry within the account of the individual arts, Hegel develops his theory of tragic "collision" (Kollision): ancient tragedy characteristically pits two substantively justified ethical powers (family/state, divine/human law) against one another in individual agents who identify wholly with one power and thereby necessarily violate the other. Sophocles' Antigone is repeatedly invoked as the paradigm instance of this structure, distinguished from modern tragedy (e.g., Shakespeare), which for Hegel turns on subjective character rather than objective ethical substance. Based on Hotho's redaction of Hegel's Berlin lectures (1820s), not an authorial manuscript. RELEVANCE: Provides Hegel's most explicit aesthetic-theoretical vocabulary ("collision," "one-sidedness," reconciliation through the fall of both parties) for what the Phenomenology dramatizes narratively; essential primary source for the tragedy-focused domain and for understanding why Hegel treats Antigone as exemplary rather than idiosyncratic. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "primary-text, Aesthetics, tragedy, Sophocles, collision, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hegel1975naturallaw,
    author = "Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich",
    title = "Natural Law: The Scientific Ways of Treating Natural Law, Its Place in Moral Philosophy, and Its Relation to the Positive Sciences of Law",
    year = "1975",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hegel's 1802 Jena essay criticizes both empiricist natural law (which cannot ground universally valid norms) and Kantian/Fichtean formalist natural law (too abstract to connect reason to actual ethical experience), arguing that a genuine philosophical science of right must instead exhibit the immanent, self-developing "ethical" totality (Sittlichkeit) of a people, illustrated in part through reflection on ancient Greek political life as an integrated whole of custom, religion, and law prior to the modern separation of state and civil society. RELEVANCE: This essay is where "ethical life" (Sittlichkeit) first emerges as a technical term opposed to abstract natural law and morality, prior to its full deployment in the Phenomenology's Antigone chapter and the Philosophy of Right; establishes the conceptual genealogy that later commentators (Molloy) trace. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "primary-text, Natural-Law-essay, Jena-writings, Sittlichkeit, Medium, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hegel1979systemethicallife,
    author = "Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich",
    editor = "Harris, H. S. and Knox, T. M.",
    title = "Hegel's System of Ethical Life and First Philosophy of Spirit",
    year = "1979",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: The untitled 1802/03 Jena manuscript "System der Sittlichkeit" attempts a systematic derivation of ethical life through stages of "absolute" and "relative" ethical life, including early treatments of family, labor, and the emergence of political community, developed independently of any explicit Antigone reference but forming the direct conceptual precursor to the Phenomenology's ethical-substance chapter. RELEVANCE: Documents the developmental stage of Hegel's ethical-life concept between the Natural Law essay and the Phenomenology, useful for tracing how the divine-law/human-law distinction later mapped onto Antigone crystallizes out of these earlier, more inchoate formulations. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "primary-text, Jena-writings, System-der-Sittlichkeit, Medium, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hegel1996lectureshistory,
    author = "Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich",
    editor = "Rockmore, Tom",
    title = "Hegel's Lectures on the History of Philosophy",
    year = "1996",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Haldane and Simson's translation (here in Rockmore's abridged edition) of Hegel's lecture cycles narrating the history of philosophy from the Greeks onward; situates Socrates', Plato's, and Aristotle's ethical and political thought within the broader "beautiful" immediacy of Greek Sittlichkeit that Hegel elsewhere (Phenomenology, Aesthetics) treats through Antigone, framing Greek philosophy's emergence as itself a symptom of the polis's unreflective ethical unity beginning to fracture. RELEVANCE: Supplies comparatively minor but relevant contextual material on how Hegel narrates the historical-philosophical significance of Greek ethical unity outside the Antigone-focused passages; useful for the polis-to-modernity domain's broader argument about periodization. POSITION: Primary source.},
    keywords = "primary-text, Lectures-History-Philosophy, Greek-philosophy, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{pinkard1994phenomenology,
    author = "Pinkard, Terry",
    title = "Hegel's Phenomenology",
    year = "1994",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: A section-by-section, chapter-by-chapter commentary reading the Phenomenology as an account of the historical development of forms of "sociality of reason" -- norms of rational accountability that only make sense within evolving social practices. The Ethical Life chapter, including its Antigone material, is read as showing how a form of immediate, unreflective social normativity (Greek Sittlichkeit) collapses because it cannot account for the individual as a subject of specifically self-conscious, reflective norm-giving. RELEVANCE: One of the standard Anglophone systematic commentaries on the Phenomenology as a whole; provides the connecting argument from the Antigone material into the subsequent "Legal Status" and "Culture" chapters that many domain-specific studies presuppose without spelling out. POSITION: Systematic commentary (social-pragmatist reading of the Phenomenology).},
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, Pinkard, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "The Phenomenology of Spirit is both one of Hegel's most widely read books and one of his most obscure. The book is the most detailed commentary on Hegel's work available. It develops an independent philosophical account of the general theory of knowledge, culture, and history presented in the Phenomenology. In a clear and straightforward style, Terry Pinkard reconstructs Hegel's theoretical philosophy and shows its connection to ethical and political theory. He sets the work in a historical context and shows the contemporary relevance of Hegel's thought for European and Anglo-American philosophers. The principal audience for the book is teachers and students of philosophy, but the great interest in Hegel's work and the clarity of Pinkard's exposition ensure that historians of ideas, political scientists, and literary theorists will also read it.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{harris1997ladder,
    author = "Harris, Henry S.",
    title = "Hegel's Ladder: Volume I: The Pilgrimage of Reason. Volume II: The Odyssey of Spirit",
    year = "1997",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: A two-volume, paragraph-by-paragraph "literal commentary" on the entire Phenomenology, the product of roughly thirty years of Harris's engagement with the text. Volume I covers Consciousness through Reason; Volume II (The Odyssey of Spirit) covers the Spirit, Religion, and Absolute Knowing chapters, including a detailed reading of the Ethical Order section and its Antigone material as the decisive first fully "actual" (as opposed to merely individual) shape of Spirit. RELEVANCE: Together with Pinkard and Hyppolite, one of the three principal book-length English-language Phenomenology commentaries synthesis writers should treat as authoritative for tracking the chapter's argumentative structure around Antigone; distinguished by its close philological attention to Hegel's German phrasing. POSITION: Systematic commentary (close textual/philological reading).},
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, Harris, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{hyppolite1974genesis,
    author = "Hyppolite, Jean",
    title = "Genesis and Structure of Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit",
    year = "1974",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hyppolite's influential mid-century French commentary (translated by Samuel Cherniak and John Heckman) reads the Phenomenology as tracing the genesis of philosophical self-consciousness out of historically embodied forms of experience. The Ethical Order chapter is read as staging the necessary self-contradiction of an ethical substance that has not yet mediated itself through subjective reflection, with Antigone and Creon each representing a "moment" (family/state) that must be superseded rather than simply preserved. RELEVANCE: The commentary that shaped much of the postwar French reception of Hegel (via Kojève and Hyppolite jointly) that subsequently influenced Lacan, Derrida, and Irigaray's readings of Antigone covered in other domains; essential for understanding the interpretive lineage those later readings work against or through. POSITION: Systematic commentary (existential-phenomenological reading, French reception tradition).},
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, Hyppolite, French-reception, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{kojeve1969introduction,
    author = "Kojève, Alexandre",
    editor = "Bloom, Allan",
    title = "Introduction to the Reading of Hegel: Lectures on the Phenomenology of Spirit",
    year = "1969",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Kojève's transcribed Paris lectures (1933-1939) recast the entire Phenomenology around the struggle for recognition and the master-slave dialectic as the engine of historical development toward the "end of history." Treats Greek ethical life and its Antigone-centered collapse as an early, still-unequal form of mutual recognition that must give way to the fully reciprocal recognition Hegel associates with the modern state. RELEVANCE: Historically decisive for twentieth-century French (and thence much Anglophone) reception of Hegel, but idiosyncratic and widely regarded by specialists as substituting Kojève's own anthropology of desire and recognition for large portions of Hegel's actual argument; should be flagged as an outlier reading rather than treated as representative when synthesis writers characterize "the" standard interpretation. POSITION: Systematic commentary, but a heterodox one (recognition/desire-centered, historicist-existentialist reading).},
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, Kojeve, recognition, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{wood1990ethical,
    author = "Wood, Allen W.",
    title = "Hegel's Ethical Thought",
    year = "1990",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: A systematic reconstruction of Hegel's practical philosophy across Abstract Right, Morality, and Ethical Life, arguing that Hegel's Sittlichkeit is best understood as a theory of self-actualization within rational social institutions rather than as either communitarian conservatism or a straightforward successor to Kantian morality. Greek ethical life is treated as the historical model of unreflective Sittlichkeit that modern ethical life both preserves (as immediate identification with one's social role) and transcends (through the addition of subjective freedom and civil society). RELEVANCE: A standard Anglophone systematic commentary on the Philosophy of Right's ethical-life doctrine, providing the conceptual vocabulary (self-actualization, one-sidedness, immediate vs. reflective identification) that many Antigone-focused readings of the Phenomenology borrow when explaining why Greek ethical life is simultaneously admirable and defective for Hegel. POSITION: Systematic commentary (self-actualization reading of Hegel's ethics).",
    keywords = "commentary, Philosophy-of-Right, ethical-life, Wood, High, INCOMPLETE, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{neuhouser2000foundations,
    author = "Neuhouser, Frederick",
    title = "Foundations of Hegel's Social Theory: Actualizing Freedom",
    year = "2000",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Hegel's theory of Sittlichkeit as a theory of "social freedom," in which rational social institutions (family, civil society, state) enable individuals to achieve a form of freedom unavailable to isolated individuals. Explicitly contrasts modern differentiated ethical life with the "immediate" and undifferentiated Sittlichkeit of the Greek polis, using the latter to explain why Hegel regards ancient ethical life as a necessary but ultimately inadequate model for modern social freedom. RELEVANCE: Along with Wood, the standard systematic account of why Hegel treats Greek Sittlichkeit (the world in which Antigone's conflict is possible) as both an object of admiration and a stage that had to be superseded by institutionally differentiated modern ethical life; essential for the polis-to-modernity domain's argument. POSITION: Systematic commentary (social-freedom reading of the Philosophy of Right).},
    keywords = "commentary, Philosophy-of-Right, Sittlichkeit, Neuhouser, social-freedom, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Frederick Neuhouser’s task is to understand the conceptions of freedom on which Hegel’s social theory rests and to show how they ground his arguments in defense of the modern social world. In doing so, the author focuses on Hegel’s most important and least understood contribution to social philosophy, the idea of “social freedom.”",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{westphal2009blackwell,
    editor = "Westphal, Kenneth R.",
    title = "The Blackwell Guide to Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit",
    year = "2009",
    doi = "10.1002/9781444306224",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: An edited collection of chapter-by-chapter guides to the Phenomenology by different specialists, providing an accessible synthesis of scholarly consensus and live disagreements on each chapter's argument, including the Spirit chapter's treatment of ethical substance and its Antigone material. RELEVANCE: A convenient cross-check against the more idiosyncratic single-author commentaries (Kojève, Hyppolite) for how mainstream Anglophone Hegel scholarship currently reconstructs the argumentative function of the Antigone material within the Phenomenology as a whole. POSITION: Edited systematic commentary (multi-author reference guide).",
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, edited-volume, Westphal, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Notes on Contributors. References. Introduction. 1. Hegel's Phenomenological Method and Analysis of Consciousness: Kenneth R. Westphal (University of Kent, Canterbury). 2. Desire, Recognition, and the Relation between Bondsman and Lord: Frederick Neuhouser (Columbia University, New York). 3. Freedom and Thought: Stoicism, Skepticism, and Unhappy Consciousness: Franco Chiereghin (University of Padua). 4. Challenge of Reason: From Certainty to Truth: Cinzia Ferrini (University of Trieste). 5. Reason Observing Nature: Cinzia Ferrini (University of Trieste). 6. Shapes of Active Reason: Law of the Heart, Retrieved Virtue, and What Really Matters: Terry Pinkard (Georgetown University, Washington, DC). 7. Ethics of Freedom: Hegel on Reason as Law-Giving and Law-Testing: David Couzens Hoy (University of California, Santa Cruz). 8. Hegel, Antigone, and Feminist Critique: Spirit of Ancient Greece: Jocelyn B. Hoy (University of California, Santa Cruz). 9. Hegel's Critique of the Enlightenment in The Struggle of the Enlightenment with Superstition: Jurgen Stolzenberg (Martin-Luther-Universitat, Halle-Wittenburg). 10. Morality in Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit: Frederick C. Beiser (Syracuse University, New York). 11. Religion, History, and Spirit in Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit: George di Giovanni (McGill University, Montreal). 12. Absolute Knowing: Allegra de Laurentiis (SUNY-Stony Brook, New York). 13. Spirit and Concrete Subjectivity in Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit: Marina F. Bykova (North Carolina State University). General Bibliography. Index of Names. Subject Index. Table of Concordances",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{moyar2021individual,
    author = "Moyar, Dean",
    title = "Individual Right and the Living Ethical Order",
    year = "2021",
    pages = "37--77",
    doi = "10.1093/oso/9780197532539.003.0002",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Hegel's account of ethical life in the Philosophy of Right systematically integrates individual right (the claims of subjective freedom developed in Abstract Right and Morality) into the "living" ethical order of family, civil society, and state, tracing how this integration reworks -- without simply repeating -- the unreflective identification of individual and ethical substance found in Greek Sittlichkeit. RELEVANCE: A recent, close textual reconstruction of exactly how modern ethical life is supposed to retain the "livingness" of Greek Sittlichkeit while adding what Greek ethical life lacked (individual right), directly relevant to the primary-text argument connecting the Phenomenology's Antigone chapter to the Philosophy of Right's systematic doctrine. POSITION: Systematic commentary on the Philosophy of Right's ethical-life doctrine.},
    keywords = "commentary, Philosophy-of-Right, ethical-life, Moyar, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "This chapter gives an account of Hegel’s early Jena critique of social contract theory and the most important elements in his account of value and justice in the Jena Phenomenology of Spirit. It begins by laying out the essentials of the most important theoretical foil for Hegel’s account: J.G. Fichte’s Foundations of Natural Right. The chapter sketches Hegel’s early attack on Fichte’s theory and the initial conception of life that is supposed to provide an antidote to individualist social contract doctrine. Hegel’s own mature treatment of recognition and life is first articulated in Chapter IV of the Phenomenology. The chapter outlines Hegel’s case for the isomorphism of self-consciousness and life in the Phenomenology and shows how that underwrites his theory of value and the emergence of value in the work of the servant. The chapter concludes with a treatment of Hegel’s account of immediate justice in the ancient Greek polis.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{katsman2023syllogism,
    author = "Katsman, Anna",
    title = "The Spirit of Ethical Life as Syllogism",
    year = "2023",
    volume = "51",
    pages = "1633--1655",
    doi = "10.1177/01914537231215697",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Hegel's account of ethical life (Sittlichkeit) in the Philosophy of Right is structured according to the logical form of the syllogism, with family, civil society, and the state functioning as interlocking "terms" that mediate universal, particular, and individual moments of ethical life, and situates the immediate, non-syllogistic unity of Greek Sittlichkeit as the historical antecedent this logical structure both formalizes and supersedes. RELEVANCE: Provides a close logical/structural reading of the Philosophy of Right's ethical-life architecture that clarifies, from the systematic side, why Hegel regards Greek ethical life (Antigone's world) as structurally "immediate" rather than syllogistically mediated. POSITION: Systematic-logical commentary on the Philosophy of Right.},
    keywords = "commentary, Philosophy-of-Right, Sittlichkeit, syllogism, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, number, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "In this essay, I take on the problem of how to explain the socio-historical dimension of practical reason in Hegel. In contrast to many contemporary socio-historical readings of Hegel, I claim that a logical concept of spirit frames Hegel’s account of the historical process through which human beings have come to know their practical agency as actualized in institutional relations of mutual recognition. On my reading, Hegel conceptualizes each shape in the ‘Spirit’ chapter of his Phenomenology of Spirit as syllogistically working out, practically and institutionally, how to mediate the demands of individual self-determination with the universal laws and customs of the community. At the same time, logical form doesn’t determine historical development, because the content of individuality, universality, and their mediation is earned only through historical experience.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@article{hoff2006lawright,
    author = "Hoff, Shannon",
    title = "Law, Right, and Forgiveness: The Remains of Antigone in the Phenomenology of Spirit",
    journal = "Philosophy Today",
    year = "2006",
    volume = "50",
    doi = "10.5840/philtoday200650supplement4",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Antigone's conflict does not simply disappear once the Phenomenology moves on to "Legal Status" and later chapters (including the account of forgiveness in "Conscience"), but that the unresolved tension she embodies persists as a structuring "remainder" within Hegel's subsequent account of law, right, and ultimately mutual forgiveness between conscientious agents. RELEVANCE: A close textual argument for tracing the Antigone material's continuing argumentative function beyond the Ethical Order chapter itself, directly useful for establishing how far into the Phenomenology's later chapters the Antigone passage's effects should be traced. POSITION: Close textual/continuity reading of the Phenomenology.},
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, Antigone, forgiveness, Hoff, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\\_CLEANED: number, pages"
}

@misc{deboer2010beyond,
    author = "de Boer, Karin",
    title = "Beyond Tragedy: Tracing the Aristophanian Subtext of Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit",
    year = "2010",
    pages = "133--151",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the Spirit chapter's argument does not end with the tragic destruction dramatized through Antigone but is completed only by the subsequent, comparatively neglected discussion of Greek comedy (echoing Aristophanes), which for Hegel marks the more thorough dissolution of immediate ethical substance than tragedy alone achieves; reconstructs this "Aristophanian subtext" as essential to understanding why Hegel treats tragedy as an incomplete rather than final verdict on Greek ethical life. RELEVANCE: Corrects a tendency in Antigone-focused secondary literature to treat the tragedy discussion as the terminus of Hegel's account of Greek ethical life's collapse, when the primary text's own argument continues through the comedy discussion; important for accurately reconstructing the full arc of chapter VI.A-B. POSITION: Close textual reading of the Phenomenology's ethical-substance argument (tragedy-to-comedy transition).},
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, comedy, Aristophanes, de-Boer, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc, METADATA\_CLEANED: doi",
    abstract = "Hegel’s account of the Greek ethical world in the Phenomenology of Spirit seems to have inspired more feminist philosophers than any other classical text. This is hardly surprising, given Hegel’s explicit reflection on the issue of gender in the sections devoted to the ethical world. Since he seems to maintain, moreover, that men and women should comply with the roles assigned to them by nature, it is no more surprising that feminist readings of this text have generally assumed the form of criticism. There is no doubt that these criticisms have played an important part in the development of feminist philosophy. It seems to me, however, that the time has come to assess critically these criticisms of Hegel themselves. In order to contribute to such a critical assessment I will, in this chapter, try to extricate Hegel’s text from some of the preconceptions that have guided feminist readings of Hegel. As I see it, these readings generally presuppose, first, that the sections of the Phenomenology devoted to Greek ethical life purport to offer an interpretation of Sophocles’ Antigone and, second, that Hegel uses the Antigone to express his own view on the issue of gender.2 In order to challenge these claims I will reconsider the texts at stake in light of the peculiar method that Hegel deploys throughout the Phenomenology. In doing so, I will largely focus on the section entitled Ethical Action and dwell in particular on its final pages. I thus hope to reconstruct the hidden subtext of these pages and, hence, to illuminate some of its most abstruse sections.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{molloy2024naturallaw,
    author = "Molloy, Seán",
    title = "Hegel's First Attempt to Re-Philosophize Natural Law: Undistorted Intuition, Dead Laws and Ethical Life in 'On the Scientific Ways of Treating Natural Law'",
    journal = "Hegel Bulletin",
    year = "2024",
    volume = "46",
    pages = "563--589",
    doi = "10.1017/hgl.2024.51",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Provides a close reading of the 1802 Natural Law essay, arguing that Hegel's critique of empiricist and formalist natural law is driven by a demand for "undistorted intuition" of ethical life as a living totality, and that this essay's introduction of "ethical life" as a technical category -- prior to any explicit engagement with Antigone -- already contains the conceptual resources later deployed in the Phenomenology and Philosophy of Right. RELEVANCE: The most direct recent scholarly treatment of the Natural Law essay specifically named in this domain's search brief; establishes the Jena-period genealogy of "Sittlichkeit" that the Phenomenology's Antigone chapter and the Philosophy of Right presuppose. POSITION: Close textual/genetic reading of the 1802 Natural Law essay.},
    keywords = "commentary, Natural-Law-essay, Jena-writings, Molloy, Medium, METADATA\\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = "Abstract Hegel’s ‘Natural Law’ essay is widely discussed but its substance and the implications of its argument are misunderstood. Hegel’s essay is most often read via other philosophers. Interpretations of this kind are useful but only illuminate those parts of Hegel’s text that intersect with other philosophers’ concerns. This article takes a different approach by focusing on the entirety of the essay and exploring the implications of its two primary arguments: firstly, that there has been a breach between philosophy and natural law; secondly, that without philosophy natural law is thrown back on its own resources, producing two schools of post-philosophical thought—empiricism and formalism—neither of which is adequate to serve as its foundation. I argue that in response to this inadequacy Hegel sublates empiricism and formalism to develop undistorted intuition as a way of thinking designed to effect the re-philosophization of natural law.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{finlayson1999conflict,
    author = "Finlayson, James Gordon",
    title = "Conflict and Reconciliation in Hegel's Theory of the Tragic",
    year = "1999",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Hegel's theory of tragedy from the Aesthetics as centered on a distinctive account of "reconciliation" (Versöhnung) achieved through, not despite, the mutual destruction of the colliding ethical powers, arguing against readings that treat Hegelian tragic reconciliation as either straightforwardly optimistic (a theodicy of history) or as simply absent; situates Antigone as the paradigm case Hegel uses to work out this structure. RELEVANCE: The most sustained analytic reconstruction of the specifically philosophical content of Hegel's theory of tragic collision and reconciliation as stated in the Aesthetics, essential primary-text scaffolding for the tragedy-focused domain of this review. POSITION: Close textual/philosophical reconstruction of the Aesthetics' theory of tragedy.},
    keywords = "commentary, Aesthetics, tragedy, reconciliation, Finlayson, High, METADATA\\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "The aims of this article is to expound and defend Hegel's theory of the tragic and to clarify his concept of reconciliation. These two aims are Related in that a widespread but misleading conception of the tragic and a common, but mistaken, understanding of Hegel' s concept of reconciliation can seem to offer mutual support. I argue that once these are properly understood we can better understand Hegel's curious readings of the Antigone by Sophocles and the Eumenides by Aeschylus. I show that the latter rather than the former better exemplifies Hegel's theory, of which I offer a qualified defense.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{stewart2016uncovering,
    author = "Stewart, Heather",
    title = "Uncovering Gender in Hegel's 'Ethical Order' in The Phenomenology of Spirit",
    year = "2016",
    howpublished = "Episteme, vol. 27",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Hegel's account of the Ethical Order chapter's gendered division of divine/human law (family/state) is not incidental to the chapter's philosophical argument but is constitutive of how Hegel conceives the necessary self-division of ethical substance, such that the gender asymmetry cannot be excised from the argument without undermining its structure. RELEVANCE: Bears directly on how far the Antigone chapter's gender coding is separable from its properly philosophical claims about ethical substance, a question presupposed by feminist-reception debates handled in another domain. POSITION: Close textual reading emphasizing the constitutive (not incidental) role of gender in the Phenomenology's ethical-substance argument. Note: Bibliographic record could not be independently verified via CrossRef; venue metadata (Episteme, vol. 27) is as reported by Semantic Scholar and should be treated with caution.",
    keywords = "commentary, Phenomenology, gender, Stewart, INCOMPLETE, Low, no-abstract"
}

@misc{speight2001hegel,
    author = "Speight, Allen",
    title = "Hegel, Literature, and the Problem of Agency",
    year = "2001",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Speight argues that Hegel's Phenomenology treats agency as fundamentally "retrospective": agents cannot know the full meaning, scope, or guilt of their deeds in advance, but only discover this through the deed's consequences, its reception, and the acts of forgiveness and judgment that follow it. The central chapter, "Tragedy and Retrospectivity: Hegel's Antigone," reads Antigone's confrontation with Creon as the paradigm case of this structure: her action is at once fully hers (an exercise of self-certain will) and only fully determined, in its ethical significance, after the fact. RELEVANCE: Supplies the dominant Anglophone philosophical-of-action framework for understanding why Hegel calls ethical action inherently guilty, directly answering this domain's key question about the relationship between ethical action, guilt, and knowledge. POSITION: Retrospectivity/agency reading.},
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, ethical-action, guilt, retrospectivity, agency, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit has attracted much attention recently from philosophers, but none of the existing English-language books on the text addresses one of the most difficult questions the book raises: Why does the Phenomenology make such rich and provocative use of literary works and genres? Allen Speight's bold contribution to the debate on the work of Hegel argues that behind Hegel's extraordinary appeal to literature in the Phenomenology lies a philosophical project concerned with understanding human agency in the modern world. It shows that Hegel looked to three literary genres - tragedy, comedy, and the Romantic novel - as offering privileged access to three moments of human agency: retrospectivity, theatricality, and forgiveness. Taking full account of the authors whom Hegel himself refers to (Sophocles, Diderot, Schlegel, Jacobi), Allen Speight has written a book with a broad appeal to both philosophers and literary theorists.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{williams1998ethics,
    author = "Williams, Robert R.",
    title = "Hegel's Ethics of Recognition",
    year = "1998",
    address = "Berkeley",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Williams offers a systematic reconstruction of recognition (Anerkennung) as the organizing concept of Hegel's practical philosophy, including a detailed treatment of the Ethical Order chapter in which the divine law/human law conflict is read as exposing a form of mutual recognition -- between family and state, and between the individual and the ethical substance -- that is still too immediate and undifferentiated to accommodate the particular individual (Antigone) it destroys. RELEVANCE: Provides the recognition-theoretic vocabulary that much subsequent scholarship on the Ethical Order chapter (including several other entries in this domain) draws on or reacts against; central for reconstructing why, on Hegel's view, the collapse of Sittlichkeit is necessary rather than merely contingent. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading, recognition-theoretic framework.",
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, recognition, ethical-substance, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "In this significant contribution to Hegel scholarship, Robert Williams develops the most comprehensive account to date of Hegel's concept of recognition ( Anerkennung ). Fichte introduced the concept of recognition as a presupposition of both Rousseau's social contract and Kant's ethics. Williams shows that Hegel appropriated the concept of recognition as the general pattern of his concept of ethical life, breaking with natural law theory yet incorporating the Aristotelian view that rights and virtues are possible only within a certain kind of community.He explores Hegel's intersubjective concept of spirit ( Geist ) as the product of affirmative mutual recognition and his conception of recognition as the right to have rights. Examining Hegel's Jena manuscripts, his Philosophy of Right , the Phenomenology of Spirit , and other works, Williams shows how the concept of recognition shapes and illumines Hegel's understandings of crime and punishment, morality, the family, the state, sovereignty, international relations, and war. A concluding chapter on the reception and reworking of the concept of recognition by contemporary thinkers including Derrida, Levinas, and Deleuze demonstrates Hegel's continuing centrality to the philosophical concerns of our age",
    abstract_source = "core"
}

@misc{pippin2008practical,
    author = "Pippin, Robert B.",
    title = "Hegel's Practical Philosophy: Rational Agency as Ethical Life",
    year = "2008",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Pippin situates Hegel's theory of ethical action -- including the claim that acting is inherently guilty because agents cannot fully anticipate or control the social meaning their deeds will acquire -- within a general theory of rational agency in which self-knowledge is always mediated by, and dependent on, social recognition and normative practice rather than being transparent to the individual will in advance. RELEVANCE: Offers the most systematic non-metaphysical, action-theoretic reconstruction of the guilt thesis at stake in this domain's second key question, treating the Antigone material as one instance of a general Hegelian claim about the retrospective, socially mediated character of agency and responsibility. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading, non-metaphysical action-theoretic framework.",
    keywords = "ethical-action, guilt, agency, rational-agency, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "This fresh and original book argues that the central questions in Hegel's practical philosophy are the central questions in modern accounts of freedom: What is freedom, or what would it be to act freely? Is it possible so to act? And how important is leading a free life? Robert Pippin argues that the core of Hegel's answers is a social theory of agency, the view that agency is not exclusively a matter of the self-relation and self-determination of an individual but requires the right sort of engagement with and recognition by others. Using a detailed analysis of key Hegelian texts, he develops this interpretation to reveal the bearing of Hegel's claims on many contemporary issues, including much-discussed core problems in the liberal democratic tradition. His important study will be valuable for all readers who are interested in Hegel's philosophy and in the modern problems of agency and freedom.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{congdon2008guilty,
    author = "Congdon, Matthew Lyons",
    title = "Hegel's Guilty Conscience: Three Forms of {Schuld} in the {Phenomenology of Spirit}",
    year = "2008",
    volume = "3",
    pages = "32--55",
    doi = "10.22329/p.v3i1.296",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Congdon traces the development of Schuld (guilt) across the entire "Spirit" chapter -- through Antigone, the French Revolution, and the confession-and-forgiveness sequence -- distinguishing "abstract guilt" (which produces only shame and punishment) from "determinate guilt" (which is productive, indebting the guilty party to others and driving further ethical development). Antigone's guilt in violating one law while fulfilling another is read as the paradigmatic instance of this productive, structurally necessary guilt. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses this domain's key question about the relationship between ethical action, guilt, and knowledge by showing how Hegel's concept of guilt changes in meaning across the chapter, connecting the Ethical Order's guilt thesis to the later, more explicitly social and political forms of guilt Hegel analyzes. POSITION: Retrospectivity/agency reading, with an explicit recognition-theoretic payoff.},
    abstract = {In what we might call its particularly Christian manifestation, "guilt" denotes the feeling or fact of having offended, the failure to uphold an ethical code. Under such terms, "guilt" connotes negative consequences: shame, punishment, and estrangement. Yet, penetrating further into its meaning and value, one finds that guilt extends beyond this narrow classification, playing a productive, necessary, and ineluctable role for recognitive sociality. This paper examines guilt as it appears in Hegel's thinking. I find that Hegel's understanding of Schuld (guilt) in the Phenomenology, undergoes a crucial development over the course of the chapter titled, "Spirit," culminating in a robust understanding of guilt that represents not a hopelessly broken bond, but a bond that awaits its fulfillment, its very incompleteness exerting a palpable pull upon the guilty party towards its fulfillment. I examine three key moments in "Spirit": Hegel's treatments of Antigone, the French Revolution, and the confession and forgiveness of evil. By comparing these moments, I distinguish between "abstract guilt," guilt that only brings about shame and punishment, and what we might call "determinate guilt": guilt that brings about action, reminds one of her/his indebtedness to the other. Understanding the development of guilt from the beginning to end of "Spirit" provides an entryway into a discussion of the social and political relevance of Hegel's conception of the subject as -- in a certain sense -- always already guilty. I go on to argue that guilt as indebtedness and responsibility only exists as embedded within an already recognitive social structure.},
    abstract_source = "semantic\_scholar",
    keywords = "ethical-action, guilt, Schuld, recognition, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, type:@article->@misc"
}

@article{sandis2010handlung,
    author = "Sandis, Constantine",
    title = "The Man Who Mistook his {Handlung} for a {Tat}: Hegel on Oedipus and Other Tragic Thebans",
    journal = "Bulletin of the Hegel Society of Great Britain",
    year = "2010",
    volume = "31",
    pages = "35--60",
    doi = "10.1017/S0263523200000057",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Sandis examines Hegel's terminological distinction, introduced in the Ethical Order chapter, between Tat (the deed considered as an objective, publicly ascribable event) and Handlung (the deed considered as intentionally owned and known by the agent), arguing that this distinction -- central to Hegel's claim that ethical action is inherently guilty even where agents act in ignorance (as with Oedipus, though the argument bears directly on Antigone's more deliberate transgression) -- is frequently conflated or mishandled by commentators. RELEVANCE: Provides the philosophy-of-action vocabulary (Tat/Handlung) needed to state precisely why, on Hegel's view, ethical action is guilty regardless of the agent's subjective intentions or knowledge, directly bearing on this domain's guilt/knowledge question. POSITION: Retrospectivity/agency reading, focused on conceptual/terminological precision.",
    keywords = "ethical-action, guilt, Handlung, Tat, Oedipus, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = "Throughout his work Hegel distinguishes between the notion of an act (Tun) from the standpoint of the agent (behaviour in so far as it relates to one's own foreknowledge, purpose, intention, and knowledge) and that of all other standpoints (e.g. legal, scientific, cultural, etc.). He terms the former Handlung (action) and the latter Tat (deed). This distinction should not be confused with the contemporary one between action and mere bodily movement. For one, both Handlung and Tat are aspects of conduct that results from the will, viz. Tun (LA 1160ff.). Moreover, Hegel's taxonomy is motivated purely by concerns relating to modes of perception. So whereas theorists such as Donald Davidson assert that all actions are events that are intentional under some description, Hegel reserves the term ‘action’ for those aspects of behaviour that are highlighted by a specific (albeit contested) set of agent-related descriptions. This is not an ontological category, since there are no such objects as actions-under-specific-descriptions (see Anscombe 1979). Sophocles's Theban Trilogy reveals the central role that these notions must play in any Hegelian understanding of tragic drama. Indeed the contrasts that matter most to Hegel's general take on both epic and tragic poetry are more closely related to the study of action than the standard theory attributed to Hegel would seem to allow. It is more fruitful, then, to incorporate Hegel's insights into such tragedies to the model of action employed by him than it is to try to make them fit whatever ‘theory’ of tragedy might appear to be hinted at in his Aesthetics.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{wenning2019tragic,
    author = "Wenning, Mario",
    title = "Tragic Recognition: Revisiting Hegel's Conception of Ethical Life",
    year = "2019",
    volume = "13",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Wenning revisits the divine-law/human-law conflict as an instance of "tragic recognition": a form of mutual recognition that succeeds only by destroying the very individuals who enact it, since Antigone and Creon can achieve genuine (if fatal) recognition of the other's law only by first refusing to recognize its legitimacy in action. RELEVANCE: Offers a recent (2019) restatement and defense of the reconciliation/systemic reading of the Ethical Order chapter, directly addressing this domain's question about how commentators reconstruct the logical structure of the divine/human law conflict. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading.},
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, recognition, ethical-life, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{bisticascocoves2005tragedy,
    author = "Bisticas-Cocoves, Marcos",
    title = "Tragedy, Comedy, and Ethical Action in Hegel's {Phenomenology of Spirit}",
    year = "2005",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Bisticas-Cocoves traces the movement of "ethical action" from the tragic one-sidedness dramatized by Antigone and Creon through to the comic self-relativization of later shapes of Spirit, arguing that Hegel's dialectic requires the tragic collision of the Ethical Order to be superseded by a form of consciousness that no longer identifies wholly and unreflectively with a single ethical power. RELEVANCE: Connects this domain's guilt/ethical-action question to the review's broader narrative of the transition from Sittlichkeit to self-alienated, reflective spirit, tracing the systematic function tragedy and comedy play in motivating that transition. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading, tragedy-to-comedy developmental account.},
    keywords = "ethical-action, tragedy, comedy, Bildung-transition, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, volume, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "For most readers of the Phenomenology of Spirit, Hegel's example ofEthical Action is taken from Sophocles' Antigone. In fact, however, Hegel provides us with a trilogy of tragic examples. The first is Sophocles' OedipusTyrannos; the second, Aeschylus's Seven against Thebes; Antigone is but the third. Further, just as a dramatic trilogy was followed by a satyr play among the ancients, ethical action's final moment is taken from Aristophanes' Ekklesiazousai. These four examples do not form a simple series where each equally expresses the truth of ethical action. Rather, they are increasingly adequate to that truth.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{vandenberge2017antigone,
    author = "van den Berge, Lukas",
    title = "Sophocles' {Antigone} and the Promise of Ethical Life: Tragic Ambiguity and the Pathologies of Reason",
    journal = "Law and Humanities",
    year = "2017",
    volume = "11",
    pages = "205--227",
    doi = "10.1080/17521483.2017.1362180",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Van den Berge argues that the widespread view of the Antigone/Creon confrontation as a clash between "divine" and "human" law is itself mistaken, building on Hegel's own insistence that both protagonists' positions are equally incomplete and equally "divine." He departs from Hegel, however, in denying that the play promises any higher ethical-life synthesis reconciling the two positions, arguing instead that the play teaches the unattainability of such harmonization. RELEVANCE: Directly engages this domain's key question of whether Hegel's reading is a philosophical appropriation, distortion, or productive rearticulation of Sophocles, using Hegel's own analytic apparatus to reach an anti-reconciliatory conclusion Hegel himself resisted. POSITION: Skeptical/anti-reconciliation reading, built from within a broadly Hegelian framework.},
    abstract = "This article aims to demonstrate that works of art and literature can provide important insights in law and justice that are hard to grasp by one-sidedly rationalist methods of academic analysis. It takes Sophocles' Antigone -- perhaps the most classical text of law and literature's familiar catalogue -- as a case in point, drawing attention to some important aspects of that play's legal epistemic relevance that are still largely overlooked. Arguing that the widespread view on the confrontation between Antigone and Creon as a clash between 'divine' and 'human' law is mistaken, the article builds on Hegel's view that the positions of both protagonists are likewise incomplete, denying elements of law and justice that are equally essential, the one being no less divine than the other. However, it departs from Hegel's analysis in maintaining that the play does not entail the promise of 'ethical life' (Sittlichkeit) as some synthesis that recognizes the specific value of both Antigone's and Creon's stances on law and justice but takes away their incompatibility. Instead, it is argued that the play teaches us that such harmonization is unattainable -- a no less valuable lesson indeed.",
    abstract_source = "semantic\_scholar",
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, tragedy, reconciliation-critique, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: number"
}

@article{schmidt2015monstrous,
    author = "Schmidt, Dennis J.",
    title = "The Monstrous, Catastrophe, and Ethical Life",
    journal = "Philosophy Today",
    year = "2015",
    volume = "59",
    pages = "61--72",
    doi = "10.5840/philtoday201412251",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Schmidt reads the destruction of Antigone's ethical world through Hegel and, in contrast, Heidegger, arguing that what Hegel treats as a necessary and ultimately productive catastrophe -- the self-destruction of an ethical substance too immediate to sustain itself -- is better understood (following Heidegger's alternative emphasis) as genuinely monstrous and without guaranteed recuperation into a higher ethical configuration. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses this domain's question about the motivation for the transition from true spirit to self-alienated spirit, offering a comparative (Hegel/Heidegger) account of why the collapse of Sittlichkeit is catastrophic rather than smoothly dialectical. POSITION: Skeptical/anti-reconciliation reading, developed through comparison with Heidegger.",
    keywords = "Bildung-transition, catastrophe, Heidegger-comparison, ethical-life, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = "It is striking that both Hegel and Heidegger turn to Sophocles's Antigone in order to give an account of the deepest roots of ethical and political life. For both, this very strange ancient Greek text exposes something decisive about the shape of the present age; despite its historical distance, its words announce something that is simultaneously impossible, ineluctable, and irrevocable for us, something that needs a response from a space of understanding not yet apparent. Both Hegel and Heidegger take this tragedy as pointing to a new sensibility, one that opens up a new and more original ethical and political order. For both, this drama that is so concerned with and saturated by death gives birth to a radically new possibility and summons the deepest natality of thought and the movement of history. The question I want to address today concerns the way in which Hegel and Heidegger-often in quite different ways-find this poetic presentation of a deep and impossible antagonism as illuminating what needs to be understood about the idiom of political and ethical life in our times.Now before beginning with that question, I do need to note something that both Hegel and Heidegger almost completely ignore; namely, that Antigone, a text drawn from a trilogy and best read in that context, lays out a quite bizarre situation-Creon is Antigone's uncle, Antigone's brothers have killed one another, the entire family kinship lines are quite blurred (mothers are also wives, brothers are also fathers, sisters are daughters), the blind are the those who see most clearly, the living are buried alive, there is murder, suicide, and fratricide, and of course this is perhaps the only play in which a corpse is a main character. In other words, roles are doubled, taboos are violated, and the living and the dead seem to trade places. One must concede that the choice of this particular text to unfold an ethical and political sensibility would, on the surface, seem a bit odd. But in a sense it is precisely this messiness that is so important for each. Nonetheless, there is a great deal played out in Antigone that neither Hegel nor Heidegger is concerned about. Neither 'reads' the play, neither sets it into any of its own contexts, and, in Hegel's case, the most significant allusions to the dramatic life of Antigone do not even mention the play, its characters, or its author by name, while in Heidegger's case there is only minimal reference to the drama at all. In short, neither Hegel nor Heidegger is really intent upon interpreting Antigone; both readily set aside too much for that to even be a possibility. And yet, for both this text assumes an unparalleled place, a pivotal moment, in their own thought. What this makes clear is that both Hegel and Heidegger turn to Antigone with a very specific intent. For each, the great achievement of Antigone is found in the way it condenses the riddles of ethical life while simultaneously showing that these conflicts emerge from a region that given ethical terms cannot grasp and that cannot be grasped by the conceptual language of philosophy. That is why both place great emphasis on the poetic character of this text.My goal is to say something about this intent in each case. For now, I will simply say this: that, in the end, neither understands Antigone as a struggle of right and wrong, good and evil, it is beyond those terms which inappropriately frame most ethical judgment; rather, they both understand the real contribution of Antigone to be found in the exposure of the deep antagonism, violence, and incommensurability that opens the space of ethical and political responsibility. Both use the word monstrous (ungeheuer) to speak of this opening, but each understands its significance in distinct ways.***Heidegger's engagement with Greek tragedy takes place almost exclusively during the thousand-and-one-year period of 1933-1946: the first reference, to Aeschylus, is in 1933, while the last is in Anaximander's Verdict in 1946. …",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{hoff2006restoring,
    author = "Hoff, Shannon",
    title = "Restoring Antigone to Ethical Life: Nature and Sexual Difference in Hegel's {Phenomenology of Spirit}",
    journal = "Owl of Minerva",
    year = "2006",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hoff argues that Hegel's account of the divine law/human law conflict, properly understood, does not simply assign women to a "natural," ahistorical family sphere; rather, the chapter's dialectic exposes how Greek Sittlichkeit itself naturalizes gender difference in a way that the subsequent development of Spirit -- and Hegel's own systematic account -- ultimately criticizes and moves beyond, suggesting Antigone's exclusion is diagnosed rather than endorsed. RELEVANCE: Bears directly on this domain's question about how the divine law/human law structure is reconstructed, showing how gender is internally constitutive of that structure rather than an incidental feature, while remaining focused on ethical-life mechanics rather than the feminist-reception debate treated as a separate domain. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading with an internal gender-critical dimension.},
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, gender, ethical-substance, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi"
}

@article{farneth2013gender,
    author = "Farneth, Molly",
    title = "Gender and the Ethical Given",
    journal = "Journal of Religious Ethics",
    year = "2013",
    volume = "41",
    pages = "643--667",
    doi = "10.1111/jore.12036",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Farneth argues that Hegel criticizes the ancient Greeks' understanding of both human law and divine law, and that this critique provides resources for challenging essentialist approaches to sex and gender: on her reading, Hegel views the Antigone/Creon conflict as tragic precisely because the gendered identities and obligations built into Greek Sittlichkeit are naturalized and withheld from critical scrutiny or revision, a condition Hegel's own dialectic exposes as untenable. RELEVANCE: Addresses this domain's core question about the structure of the divine law/human law conflict from a religious-ethics standpoint, showing how the "given" (rather than self-consciously chosen or revisable) character of ethical norms in the ancient world is itself what Hegel's chapter diagnoses as the source of tragic conflict. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading with an internal gender-critical dimension.},
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, gender, ethical-given, religious-ethics, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = "Abstract G. W. F. Hegel's discussion of the Antigone in the Phenomenology of Spirit has provoked ongoing debate about his views on gender. This essay offers an interpretation of Hegel as condemning social arrangements that take the authoritativeness of identities and obligations to be natural or merely given. Hegel criticizes the ancient Greeks' understanding of both the human law and the divine law; in so doing, he provides resources for a critique of essentialist approaches to sex and gender. On this interpretation, Hegel views the conflict between Antigone and Creon as tragic because the gendered identities and obligations inherent to Greek Sittlichkeit are naturalized and withheld from scrutiny and revision. In the conclusion, I suggest how Hegel's criticisms pose a challenge to certain approaches to religious ethics.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{orozco2024conciencia,
    author = "Orozco Garibay, Jos\'{e} Manuel",
    title = "La Conciencia \'{E}tica Como Acci\'{o}n Humana y Divina en la {Fenomenolog\'{i}a del esp\'{i}ritu} de Hegel: Culpa y Destino, \textquestiondown{}Somos Fatalmente Culpables?",
    year = "2024",
    volume = "22",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Orozco Garibay argues that Hegel's opposition of two duties -- each of which compels action, and each of which is transgressed by fulfilling the other -- makes fate, rather than culpable choice, condemn Antigone to violate one law while fulfilling the other; ethical substance is broken by the very structure of the conflict, raising the question of whether such guilt is avoidable in principle or is instead a "fatal" (unavoidable) feature of finite ethical agency as such. RELEVANCE: Offers a recent (2024), Spanish-language contribution to this domain's central guilt/knowledge question, framing the debate explicitly around whether Antigone's culpability is contingent or structurally necessary given the divided character of ethical substance. POSITION: Retrospectivity/agency reading, focused on the fate/guilt relationship.},
    abstract = "It is usually thought that guilt comes from a transgression of the norm or law. The universal reason determines to act according to the duty of a subject withdrawn within himself. The action that emanates from obedience to the law is the moral virtue of the singular. But Hegel proposes the tragedy of an opposition between two duties that compel to act, at the same time, according to them. However, in obeying one of the laws the other is transgressed. Thus, the virtue of following the law is, at the same time, transgression with respect to the other opposing law. Fate condemns Antigone to violate the law of reason, acting, however, in accordance with her duty. The ethical substance is broken, how to avoid guilt?",
    abstract_source = "semantic\_scholar",
    keywords = "ethical-action, guilt, fate, Spanish-language, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{mudde2009risky,
    author = "Mudde, Anna",
    title = "Risky Subjectivity: Antigone, Action, and Universal Trespass",
    year = "2009",
    volume = "32",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Mudde reads Antigone's action as an exercise of "risky subjectivity" -- a self-certain deed undertaken in full awareness that it necessarily "trespasses" against a universal (human) law even as it fulfills another (divine) universal, arguing that this structural, universal character of trespass is what makes Antigone's guilt inescapable rather than the product of individual moral failing. RELEVANCE: Contributes to this domain's guilt/knowledge question by emphasizing the risk and self-certainty built into Antigone's action, complementing Sandis's and Congdon's more conceptual treatments of the Tat/Handlung and Schuld distinctions with an account centered on subjectivity and risk. POSITION: Retrospectivity/agency reading, focused on subjectivity and risk.},
    keywords = "ethical-action, guilt, subjectivity, trespass, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{tralau2005tragedy,
    author = "Tralau, Johan",
    title = "Tragedy as Political Theory: The Self-Destruction of Antigone's Laws",
    year = "2005",
    volume = "26",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Tralau attempts to rehabilitate Hegel's claim that tragedy involves mutual guilt on the part of the adversaries, arguing that a political-theoretic reading of the play must proceed differently than readings focused solely on ethical or juridical categories: the "self-destruction" of Antigone's and Creon's respective laws is best understood as a lesson about the structural instability of any political order that cannot accommodate both principles simultaneously. RELEVANCE: Engages this domain's question about the logical/dialectical structure of the divine law/human law conflict from a political-theory rather than ethics-of-agency standpoint, testing whether Hegel's mutual-guilt thesis can be defended as political theory rather than only as moral psychology. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading, defended on political-theoretic rather than recognition-theoretic grounds.},
    keywords = "divine-law-human-law, political-theory, mutual-guilt, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, pages, type:@article->@misc"
}

@article{burke2010firstperson,
    author = "Burke, Victoria I.",
    title = "Hegel, {Antigone}, and First-Person Authority",
    journal = "Philosophy and Literature",
    year = "2010",
    volume = "34",
    pages = "373--380",
    doi = "10.1353/phl.2010.0002",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Burke argues that Hegel places Antigone at the summit of his hierarchy of the arts, at the juncture between aesthetics and the philosophy of religion, because of her distinctive first-person authority: her self-certainty about what she is called upon to do (bury her brother) is not undermined by, but is instead constitutive of, the tragic collision with Creon's equally self-certain human law. RELEVANCE: Bears on this domain's guilt/knowledge question by emphasizing the specifically first-personal, self-certain character of Antigone's ethical knowledge, complementing the more general treatments of the Tat/Handlung and Schuld distinctions found elsewhere in this domain. POSITION: Retrospectivity/agency reading, focused on self-certainty and first-person authority.",
    keywords = "ethical-action, first-person-authority, self-certainty, Low, METADATA\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = "Hegel thought Sophocles' Antigone was the finest tragedy, and he put drama atop his hierarchy of the arts, Antigone is thus located precisely at the point where his system transitions from aesthetics to the philosophy of religion. I argue that the Antigone owes its place in Hegel's hierarchy to its focus on Antigone's uncanny self-certainty. Antigone's stance of certainty and the accompanying readiness for death render her the supreme uncanny, an identity with the truth in a world whose universal ethical substance provides (like our own) only fragmented normative clarity.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{khatab2006ethical,
    author = "Khatab, Rhonda",
    title = "Ethical Consciousness in the Spirit of Tragedy: Hegel's {Antigone}",
    year = "2006",
    volume = "11",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Khatab reconstructs Hegel's account of "ethical consciousness" as it is dramatized through Antigone's tragic action, tracing how the immediate, unreflective certainty of ethical substance is exposed as one-sided precisely through the tragic collision the play stages. RELEVANCE: Provides a further reconstruction of the ethical-substance-in-conflict structure central to this domain's first key question, situating the tragedy specifically within Hegel's account of the development of consciousness. POSITION: Reconciliation/systemic reading, framed through the development of consciousness.},
    keywords = "ethical-substance, tragedy, consciousness, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, pages, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{finlayson1997beyond,
    author = "Finlayson, James Gordon",
    title = "Beyond the {Antigone} Complex: A Reply to Jay Bernstein",
    year = "1997",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Finlayson responds directly to J.M. Bernstein's influential psychoanalytically inflected critique of Hegel's reading of Antigone (the "Antigone complex"), defending aspects of Hegel's reconciliatory account of ethical action against the charge that it papers over a properly tragic, non-recuperable remainder in the Sophoclean material. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses this domain's fourth key question -- whether Hegel's reading is a philosophical appropriation, distortion, or productive rearticulation of Sophocles -- by staging an explicit defense-and-critique exchange (Bernstein versus Finlayson) that other entries in this domain either presuppose or extend. POSITION: Defense of the reconciliation reading against a skeptical, psychoanalytically informed critique.},
    keywords = "reconciliation-critique, Bernstein-debate, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, pages, publisher, doi, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{irigaray1985eternal,
    author = "Irigaray, Luce",
    title = "The Eternal Irony of the Community",
    year = "1985",
    address = "Ithaca, NY",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: In this reading of Hegel's chapter on the ethical order in the Phenomenology of Spirit, Irigaray argues that Hegel's designation of "womankind" as the "everlasting irony of the community" exposes a structural asymmetry in his account of mutual recognition: woman is assigned an unindividuated, purely natural relation to particularity (blood-kinship, the dead) that never permits her entry into the universal on the same terms as man. Using a mimetic, quasi-parodic style of close reading, Irigaray argues that the text's own contradictions (its need for and simultaneous exclusion of the feminine) betray a phallogocentric economy that cannot be resolved by Hegel's dialectic on its own terms. RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text for the entire gender/kinship reception of Hegel's Antigone; virtually every subsequent essay in this domain (Oliver, Chanter, Changfoot, Benhabib, De Boer) positions itself with or against Irigaray's reading. It directly answers the domain's first key question regarding Hegel's positioning of womankind and confinement of women to family/particularity. POSITION: Foundational continental-feminist critique; establishes the "essentialist/self-undermining Hegel" pole of the debate.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Irigaray, Sittlichkeit, sexual-difference, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@misc{butler2000antigones,
    author = "Butler, Judith",
    title = "Antigone's Claim: Kinship Between Life and Death",
    year = "2000",
    address = "New York",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Butler rereads Sophocles' Antigone against both Hegel's and Lacan's appropriations, arguing that Antigone's own position — sister devoted to an irreplaceable brother, daughter of an incestuous union, one who acts with a "masculine" defiance while invoking kinship obligation — cannot be assimilated to either Hegel's opposition of family/particularity to state/universality or Lacan's placement of kinship in a transcendent symbolic order dissociated from the social. Antigone instead occupies an unstable limit that denaturalizes the presumed distinction between kinship and the political, showing kinship to be an open, contestable site of normativity rather than a natural prepolitical given. RELEVANCE: The second foundational text for this domain; directly answers the key question on Butler's rereading of kinship, sister-brother relations, and the limits of "the political" against Hegel and Lacan. It reframes the Irigaray-initiated debate in terms of gender performativity, incest, and the critique of compulsory kinship norms rather than sexual difference as such, and has generated its own extensive reception (Lloyd, Elden, Honig). POSITION: Post-structuralist/psychoanalytic feminist rereading that displaces rather than simply inverts the Hegel-Lacan account of kinship and the political.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Butler, Lacan, kinship, gender-performativity, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{mills1996feminist,
    editor = "Mills, Patricia Jagentowicz",
    title = "Feminist Interpretations of G.W.F. Hegel",
    year = "1996",
    address = "University Park, PA",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This edited collection gathers essays (including contributions by Irigaray, Carla Lonzi, Seyla Benhabib, Mary O'Brien, and Mills herself) that read Hegel's dialectical theory both with and against the grain of gender, going beyond and behind Simone de Beauvoir's earlier creative appropriation of Hegel in The Second Sex. Mills's own introductory and framing essays argue that Hegel misrepresents women's experience in the Greek ethical community — symbolized by Antigone — as unreflective and incapable of enduring self-conscious ethical conflict, tracing this to Hegel's ranking of human law/masculinity above divine law/femininity. RELEVANCE: This is the field-defining collection for feminist engagement with Hegel and directly frames the domain's central debate (essentialist critique vs. dialectical defense); nearly every later essay surveyed in this domain (Changfoot, Wildemann Kane) explicitly positions itself relative to Mills's framing, and the 2010 Hutchings/Pulkkinen collection is explicitly conceived as its successor. POSITION: Anchors the "second-generation" feminist critique of Hegel, extending and diversifying Irigaray's opening intervention across multiple theoretical idioms (psychoanalytic, critical-theoretical, Marxist-feminist).},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, edited-collection, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@article{oliver1996antigones,
    author = "Oliver, Kelly",
    title = "Antigone's Ghost: Undoing Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit",
    journal = "Hypatia",
    year = "1996",
    volume = "11",
    pages = "67--90",
    doi = "10.1111/j.1527-2001.1996.tb00507.x",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Oliver argues that Hegel's discussion of the family in the "Ethical Order" section of the Phenomenology undermines the entire project of the book. Hegel's project demands that every element of consciousness be conceptualizable, and yet woman, an essential but unconscious element of consciousness, is in principle unconceptualizable within Hegel's own terms — an internal aporia rather than a mere omission. The essay closes by relating this discussion to contemporary debates over family values. RELEVANCE: Provides a rigorous internalist version of the "self-undermining Hegel" position, complementing Irigaray's more performative/mimetic critique with a systematic argument about what Hegel's dialectic can and cannot conceptualize; useful for assessing whether Hegel's gender account is essentialist or internally self-undermining (a key domain question). POSITION: Internal-critique/deconstructive feminist reading — the aporia is located within Hegel's own systematic requirements, not imported from outside.},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, Phenomenology-of-Spirit, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = {This essay argues that Hegel's discussion of the family in "The Ethical Order" section of Phenomenology of Spirit undermines the entire project of that text. Hegel's project demands that every element of consciousness be conceptualizable, and yet, woman, an essential unconscious element of consciousness, is in principle unconceptualizable. The end of the essay attempts to relate Hegel's discussion of the family to contemporary discussions of family values.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{benhabib2002hegel,
    author = "Benhabib, Seyla",
    title = "On Hegel, Women, and Irony",
    year = "2002",
    address = "Oxford",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Benhabib examines Hegel's designation of women as the "everlasting irony of the community," situating it within Hegel's broader account of the transition from the Greek ethical order to modern civil society and the state. She argues that Hegel's irony-figure captures a genuine structural tension (women's exclusion from the political public sphere as the price of the state's universality) even as it forecloses any positive political role for women within Hegel's own system. RELEVANCE: A widely cited critical-theoretical treatment that mediates between Irigaray's more polemical reading and closer textual-historical readings (De Boer, Changfoot); useful for the domain's question on whether Hegel's account is essentialist, historically contextual, or self-undermining. POSITION: Critical-theory (Frankfurt School-inflected) feminist reading, emphasizing the political-historical stakes of Hegel's gendering of ethical life over purely psychoanalytic or deconstructive registers.},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, critical-theory, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@article{ravven1988hegel,
    author = "Ravven, Heidi M.",
    title = "Has Hegel Anything to Say to Feminists?",
    journal = "The Owl of Minerva",
    year = "1988",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Ravven offers what she presents as a new angle on Hegel's concepts of woman and the family, arguing that despite Hegel's explicit prejudices, his account of the historic family as a social system whose inherent contradictions drive its own transcendence contains resources for a feminist theory of human liberation — specifically, an account of the family and the Greek city as unindividuated, harmonious communities that consciousness must move beyond. RELEVANCE: An early, programmatic statement of the question this domain investigates directly — whether Hegel's gender account, despite its surface misogyny, can be recuperated for feminist purposes — and functions as a touchstone that later contributors (Wildemann Kane, Changfoot) explicitly build on or depart from. POSITION: Early "recuperative" feminist reading, distinct from both the Irigarayan rejection and later purely internalist critiques (Oliver).},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, family, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: volume, number, pages, doi"
}

@misc{chanter2011antigones,
    author = "Chanter, Tina",
    title = "Antigone's Exemplarity: Irigaray, Hegel, and Excluded Grounds as Constitutive of Feminist Theory",
    year = "2011",
    address = "Albany, NY",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Chanter argues that even as Irigaray pursues the question of how sexual difference is systematically excluded from philosophy, her own recurring reliance on Antigone as exemplary figure recapitulates the kind of universalism her project is meant to combat: Irigaray's use of Antigone remains ensconced in judgments that close down inquiry beyond sexual difference. Chanter calls for opening sexual difference to other axes of difference, especially race, so it does not itself become a totalizing discourse. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's clearest instance of an internal critique of the Irigaray-Butler line from within feminist theory itself — directly answering the key question about responses the Irigaray line has received, in this case from an author sympathetic to but critical of Irigaray's method. POSITION: Intersectional feminist critique of sexual-difference feminism's own universalizing tendencies, anticipating later race-conscious readings of the Antigone reception.",
    keywords = "Irigaray, Hegel, race, sexual-difference, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, pages, doi, type:@incollection->@misc"
}

@article{changfoot2002hegels,
    author = "Changfoot, Nadine",
    title = "Hegel's Antigone: A Response to the Feminist Critique",
    journal = "The Owl of Minerva",
    year = "2002",
    volume = "33",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Changfoot reconstructs the feminist critique of Hegel's Antigone as advanced by Mills and Irigaray — that Hegel misrepresents women's experience of the Greek community as unreflective and incapable of enduring ethical conflict, and that his ranking of human law/masculinity over divine law/femininity legitimates domination — and then mounts a direct response defending Hegel's dialectical logic, arguing the critique underestimates the self-undermining, non-static character of Hegel's own conception of ethical substance. RELEVANCE: The clearest direct defense-of-Hegel response to the Mills/Irigaray line found in this search, addressing the domain's question about what responses the Irigaray-Butler line has received from defenders of Hegel. POSITION: Dialectical-defense reading, explicitly framed as a rebuttal to the essentialist-critique position represented by Mills and Irigaray.",
    keywords = "Hegel, Irigaray, feminist-philosophy, Antigone, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: number, pages, doi"
}

@article{deboer2003hegels,
    author = "de Boer, Karin",
    title = "Hegel's Antigone and the Dialectics of Sexual Difference",
    journal = "Philosophy Today",
    year = "2003",
    volume = "47",
    pages = "140--146",
    doi = "10.5840/PHILTODAY200347SUPPLEMENT18",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: De Boer offers a close textual reading of the "ethical action" section of the Phenomenology, arguing that the thrust of Hegel's treatment of Greek culture cannot be adequately grasped by focusing narrowly on his references to Sophocles' Antigone or to Greek tragedy in general; instead the dialectics of sexual difference in this section need to be situated within Hegel's broader argument about the transition from the ethical order to legal/civil status. RELEVANCE: Offers a scholarly, historically contextualizing counterweight to more polemical feminist readings, useful for assessing the domain's question of whether Hegel's gender account is best read as essentialist, historically contextual, or self-undermining. POSITION: Historicizing Hegel-scholarship response that resists both straightforward feminist condemnation and straightforward defense, insisting on textual precision about what the sexual-difference passages are actually doing.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Antigone, sexual-difference, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@misc{lloyd2005butler,
    author = "Lloyd, Moya",
    title = "Butler, Antigone and the State",
    year = "2005",
    volume = "4",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Lloyd examines Butler's turn to Antigone as a resource for theorizing a form of (feminist) resistance to the state that operates from within civil society rather than through it, in keeping with Butler's broader suspicion of enlisting state support for political demands. Lloyd traces the tension between the state's purported efficacy and Butler's theory of resignification, and highlights how Creon's failure to compel Antigone's obedience is underexamined in Butler's own reading, despite its centrality to the formation of state authority. RELEVANCE: Extends and interrogates Butler's account of kinship's relation to the political from within political theory, directly relevant to the domain's question on how Butler's rereading addresses the limits of "the political." POSITION: Sympathetic-critical extension of Butler, focused on the underdeveloped role of state/sovereign power (Creon) in Antigone's Claim.},
    keywords = "Butler, Antigone, state, political-theory, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, pages, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{elden2005place,
    author = "Elden, Stuart",
    title = "The Place of The Polis: Political Blindness in Judith Butler's Antigone's Claim",
    year = "2005",
    volume = "8",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Elden argues that Butler's reading of Antigone's Claim exhibits a "political blindness" toward the polis as a specific site and form of political organization, contending that Butler collapses or underspecifies the difference between household (oikos) and city (polis) that is central to what is actually at stake in Sophocles' play and in Hegel's own treatment of it. RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's sharpest critique of Butler from a political-theoretical rather than psychoanalytic or feminist-internal standpoint, directly answering the key question about critiques the Irigaray-Butler line has received. POSITION: Political-theoretical critique of Butler that implicitly defends a more historically and spatially specific (Hegelian/classical) understanding of the polis against what it sees as Butler's underspecified treatment of place and politics.},
    keywords = "Butler, Antigone, polis, political-theory, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{honig2013antigone,
    author = "Honig, Bonnie",
    title = "Antigone, Interrupted",
    year = "2013",
    address = "Cambridge",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Honig intervenes in the Hegel-Lacan-Butler lineage of Antigone criticism by arguing that feminist theory's "turn to Antigone" has focused almost exclusively on Antigone's relation to Creon, Polynices, and the state, neglecting the sister-sister relation between Antigone and Ismene. Recovering this relation, Honig develops an account of "sororal" conspiracy, mourning, and agonism that complicates readings (including Butler's) organized around individual heroism and death-driven defiance, arguing instead for a "counter-sovereignty" reading that emphasizes equality in life rather than equal dignity in death. RELEVANCE: Directly extends and complicates the domain's central Butler-reception debate by introducing the Ismene relation as an underexplored resource for feminist kinship theory, addressing the key question of how the field has developed extensions beyond the initial Irigaray/Butler framing. POSITION: Agonistic-democratic feminist extension of the Antigone literature, explicitly positioned as a corrective supplement to (not simple rejection of) Butler's reading.},
    keywords = "Antigone, Butler, kinship, sisterhood, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Sophocles' Antigone is a touchstone in democratic, feminist and legal theory, and possibly the most commented upon play in the history of philosophy and political theory. Bonnie Honig's rereading of it therefore involves intervening in a host of literatures and unsettling many of their governing assumptions. Exploring the power of Antigone in a variety of political, cultural, and theoretical settings, Honig identifies the 'Antigone-effect' -- which moves those who enlist Antigone for their politics from activism into lamentation. She argues that Antigone's own lamentations can be seen not just as signs of dissidence but rather as markers of a rival world view with its own sovereignty and vitality. Honig argues that the play does not offer simply a model for resistance politics or 'equal dignity in death', but a more positive politics of counter-sovereignty and solidarity which emphasizes equality in life.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{hutchings2010hegels,
    editor = "Hutchings, Kimberly and Pulkkinen, Tuija",
    title = "Hegel's Philosophy and Feminist Thought: Beyond Antigone?",
    year = "2010",
    address = "New York",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This collection gathers a new generation of feminist readings of Hegel (including contributions from Tuija Pulkkinen, Joanna Hodge, Tina Chanter, Kimberly Hutchings, and a reprint of Judith Butler's "Longing for Recognition") that explicitly ask whether feminist engagement with Hegel can or should move beyond the Antigone paradigm that had structured the field since Irigaray. Per the NDPR review used to source this abstract, the volume's Part I stages an explicit disagreement over how to read Hegel's dialectic itself: some contributors (Pulkkinen, following Malabou and Nancy) read the Aufhebung as a resource compatible with feminist and deconstructive thought, while others (Hodge, following Derrida) read it as the epitome of a masculine metaphysical totalization that Hegel's system cannot escape; Part II turns to Hegel's specific texts on women and gender and largely concludes that the anti-feminist elements are grounded in, rather than incidental to, other parts of his system. RELEVANCE: Represents a self-conscious stock-taking of the field this domain surveys, directly relevant to assessing where the Irigaray-Butler debate stood roughly a decade after Butler's Antigone's Claim and what directions (beyond Antigone) subsequent scholarship has proposed; explicitly conceived as a successor volume to Mills's 1996 collection. POSITION: Field-survey/programmatic collection marking a transitional moment between the classic Irigaray-Butler debate and more diversified contemporary approaches (represented later by Majewska, Barnaba); internally divided between essentialist-critique and dialectical-defense readings of Hegel rather than adjudicating between them.},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, edited-collection, Antigone, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Appearing some fifteen years after Patricia Mills's Feminist Interpretations of Hegel, this volume shows how far feminist scholarship on Hegel has progressed in that time. The anthology offers a diverse yet intersecting set of essays addressing both the significance of Hegel for feminist thought and the problems that his account of gender poses for feminists. The book is divided into two equal parts. The five essays of Part I engage the broad question of the relevance of Hegel for feminism, and those in Part II offer frameworks for interpreting Hegel's specific writings on women and gender in the Phenomenology of Spirit and Elements of the Philosophy of Right. The book concludes with the transcription of a brief e-mail discussion among Hutchings, Pulkkinen, Nancy Bauer, and Alison Stone on the significance of Hegel for feminist politics.",
    abstract_source = "ndpr"
}

@misc{wildemannkane2015hegel,
    author = "Wildemann Kane, Laura",
    title = "On Hegel, Women, and the Foundation of Ethical Life: Why Gender Doesn't Belong in the Family",
    year = "2015",
    volume = "44",
    pages = "1--17",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Wildemann Kane argues that although feminist philosophers are right to criticize Hegel's explicit prejudices against women — his reduction of women to physiology to explain their subordinate social role — this gendering is not required by, and in fact disrupts, Hegel's own dialectical account of the family and spirit. The gendering of family roles is thus arbitrary relative to Hegel's own systematic commitments rather than a necessary consequence of them. RELEVANCE: A recent (post-2015) contribution to the "dialectical defense" position that directly engages the domain's question about whether Hegel's gender account is essentialist or internally self-undermining, arguing for a third option: contingent and removable without damage to the system. POSITION: Revisionist-immanent-critique position, closer to Wildemann Kane's own reconstruction of Hegel than to either Irigaray's rejection or Ravven's recuperation.},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, family, gender, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, type:@article->@misc"
}

@misc{majewska2022slave,
    author = "Majewska, Ewa",
    title = "The Slave, Antigone and the Housewife: Hegel's Dialectics of the Weak",
    year = "2022",
    volume = "43",
    abstract = {This article moves across the wide spectrum of feminist interpretations of Hegel, starting with Carla Lonzi and revisiting the queer analysis of Judith Butler, in order to re-interpret the famous figure of "Unhappy Consciousness." From a feminist perspective, these passages in Phenomenology of Spirit should be read as a re-evaluation of the care and reproductive labour, which the Subject experiences as miserably repetitive and mundane, at the stage of dialectics focused on symbolic realm of recognition. The dialectics of the weak can be established based on an in-depth re-evaluation of the material, life maintaining activities traditionally neglected in the discussions of Hegel's legacy. Here these marginalized elements of the Subject's lived experience are taken into account, thus allowing the introduction of the Housewife into the dialectical process.},
    abstract_source = "s2",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Majewska surveys the feminist interpretive tradition on Hegel from Carla Lonzi through Butler and proposes reading the "Unhappy Consciousness" figure through a materialist-feminist lens centered on reproductive and care labor, introducing the figure of the "Housewife" alongside the Slave and Antigone as a third neglected position within Hegel's dialectic of recognition. RELEVANCE: A recent (2022) materialist-feminist extension of the domain's debates that moves beyond the primarily psychoanalytic/deconstructive idiom of Irigaray and Butler toward questions of labor and social reproduction, broadening the range of "beyond Antigone" responses surveyed in this domain. POSITION: Marxist-feminist rereading that reframes the Antigone-centered debate around reproductive labor rather than sexual difference or kinship as such.},
    keywords = "Hegel, feminist-philosophy, Marxist-feminism, Unhappy-Consciousness, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, doi, type:@article->@misc"
}

@article{barnaba2025hegel,
    author = "Barnaba, Martina",
    title = "Hegel and Simone de Beauvoir on Second Nature and Gender",
    journal = "Hegel Bulletin",
    year = "2025",
    volume = "46",
    abstract = "This contribution aims to relate an important topic of the Hegelian philosophy, that of second nature, to the gender question developed by Simone de Beauvoir. The core of the emancipation process described in The Second Sex lies in Beauvoir's revolutionary idea of the artificial character of gender: the latter belongs to the culturally constructed sphere of social norms and not to mere fixed nature. In this assumption the French philosopher seems to recover the Hegelian theory of second nature: Hegel believes that through an individual and social Bildungsprozess, subjects liberate themselves from the immediate level of natural necessity and reach the free horizon of spiritual existence, in which they become self-conscious actors. Beauvoir accepts in her own existentialist view this extra-natural becoming and realizes that also gender participates in it: women are not by nature 'immanent' creatures that lack 'transcendence'. Hegel, however, does not recognize the second nature of gender and falls into that same essentialism, denounced by Beauvoir, which relegates the woman to the biological plane, thus excluding her from the dialectic of second nature and self-consciousness. A dialectical solution is presented in both thinkers, whose work aims to conciliate spirit and nature beyond any Cartesian dualism.",
    abstract_source = "s2",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Barnaba compares Hegel's and Beauvoir's respective uses of the concept of "second nature" to theorize gender, arguing that Beauvoir's appropriation and revision of the Hegelian category in The Second Sex offers a distinctive model for thinking gender as neither purely natural nor purely arbitrary — a middle path between biological determinism and radical social constructionism that remains indebted to, but critically transforms, Hegel's framework, since Hegel himself does not extend the second-nature/self-consciousness dialectic to gender and thus falls into the essentialism Beauvoir diagnoses. RELEVANCE: The most recent contribution located in this search, extending the domain's debate via comparative reading of Hegel and Beauvoir on second nature; relevant to the key question of whether Hegel's account of gender is essentialist, historically contextual, or self-undermining, since Barnaba treats "second nature" as precisely the conceptual site where this ambiguity plays out. POSITION: Comparative Hegel-Beauvoir scholarship, situating the sexual-difference debate within the broader nature/second-nature distinction rather than the Antigone reading alone.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Beauvoir, second-nature, gender, Low, METADATA\_CLEANED: number, pages, doi"
}

@article{peters2011theory,
    author = "Peters, Julia",
    title = "A Theory of Tragic Experience According to Hegel",
    journal = "European Journal of Philosophy",
    year = "2011",
    volume = "19",
    pages = "85--106",
    doi = "10.1111/j.1468-0378.2009.00373.x",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Peters argues that Hegel's theory of tragedy is usually read as a theory of the objective ethical powers in collision--and of the rationality that drives and resolves such conflicts--but that this reading struggles to account for the phenomenon of tragic experience itself, especially the suffering of the tragic protagonist. She derives an alternative theory of tragic experience from Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit, arguing this Hegelian theory of tragic experience complements rather than challenges Hegel's theory of objective tragic conflict. RELEVANCE: Directly frames the central interpretive question for this domain: does Hegel's emphasis on objective collision and rational reconciliation leave room for the subjective, experiential dimension of tragedy that later theorists (Nietzsche in particular) make central? Useful for assessing whether Hegel's Antigone reading is best understood as a logic of ethical substances or as also engaging Antigone's and Creon's suffering as such. POSITION: A revisionist defense of Hegel against the charge that his rationalist model of tragedy cannot accommodate tragic suffering.",
    keywords = "Hegel, tragic-theory, tragic-experience, reconciliation, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: number",
    abstract = "Abstract: Hegel's theory of tragedy is often considered to be primarily a theory of the objective powers involved in tragic conflicts—for Hegel, these are paradigmatically competing ethical notions—and of the rationality which underlies and drives such conflicts. Such a view follows naturally from a close reading of Hegel's discussion of classical Greek tragedy in his Lectures on Aesthetics. However, this view gives rise to the question of whether Hegel's theory of tragedy can account for the significance of tragic experience, in particular the experience of tragic suffering; it has been argued repeatedly that it cannot. In contrast, I want to suggest in this paper that a theory of tragic experience can be derived from Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit. This Hegelian theory of tragic experience, I argue, should be understood as complementing rather than challenging Hegel's theory of objective tragic conflict.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{cooper2016representation,
    author = "Cooper, Andrew",
    title = "The Representation of an Action: Tragedy between Kant and Hegel",
    journal = "European Journal of Philosophy",
    year = "2017",
    volume = "25",
    pages = "573--594",
    doi = "10.1111/ejop.12166",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Cooper examines the polarized critical reception of Hegel's theory of tragedy--between those who see it as abandoning Kant's critical insights for pre-critical metaphysics, and those who see Hegel as allowing philosophy to be genuinely transformed by tragic experience. He argues the revisionist position rightly credits Hegel's critique of Kant's theoretical assumptions but underestimates the practical importance of Kant's separation of knowledge and aesthetics, and proposes an alternative account of tragedy as representing an action that is internally unified yet irreducible to theory. RELEVANCE: Situates Hegel's theory of tragedy within the Kant-to-Hegel aesthetic trajectory rather than only within the later German tradition, clarifying what is specifically "speculative" (as opposed to merely critical or reflective) about Hegel's treatment of tragic action and therefore about his reading of Antigone as enacting rather than merely representing ethical conflict. POSITION: A mediating position that credits Hegel's critique of Kant while defending the autonomy of aesthetic experience against full absorption into speculative philosophy.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Kant, tragic-theory, aesthetics, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: number, year:2016->2017",
    abstract = "Hegel's theory of tragedy has polarized critics. In the past, many philosophers have claimed that Hegel's theory of tragedy removes Kant's critical insights and returns to pre-critical metaphysics. More recently, several have argued that Hegel does not break faith with tragic experience but allows philosophy to be transformed by tragedy. In this paper I examine the strength of this revised position. First I show that it identifies Hegel's insightful critique of Kant's theoretical assumptions. Yet I then argue that it fails to note the practical importance of Kant's separation of knowledge and aesthetics. I propose an alternative approach to tragedy that builds from the revised view and yet maintains the autonomy of aesthetics. Tragedy represents an action, a set of events that are internally unified and yet cannot be reduced to theory. This is to say that tragedy confronts us with an aesthetic sphere of making and doing that, while constrained, is incessantly open and free.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@article{falkenstern2018hegel,
    author = "Falkenstern, Rachel",
    title = "Hegel on Sophocles' \emph{Oedipus the King} and the Moral Accountability of Ancient Tragic Heroes",
    journal = "Hegel Bulletin",
    year = "2020",
    volume = "41",
    pages = "159--176",
    doi = "10.1017/hgl.2018.1",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Falkenstern argues that Hegel's historically-inflected account of subjectivity and agency is essential to understanding his theory of tragedy, using Oedipus the King as her test case. Although Hegel's theory of ancient tragedy is often described as a conflict between ethical spheres embodied in two individuals, she shows that the conflict in Oedipus is instead between Oedipus's deeds and his later knowledge of what has actually occurred, and argues this seemingly subjective conflict is in keeping with Hegel's theory. Further, while Hegel sees Oedipus as wrong to take full moral accountability for the consequences of his deeds, this very taking-on of responsibility is, for Hegel, the correct action for a tragic hero, and what renders Oedipus timelessly and tragically heroic rather than a mere victim of fate. RELEVANCE: Extends the general theory of tragic collision and reconciliation to a Sophoclean case distinct from Antigone, showing how Hegel's account of historically developing subjectivity and moral accountability operates across different tragic heroes--useful for isolating what in Hegel's Antigone reading is specific to that play versus what belongs to his general theory of the ancient tragic hero. POSITION: A close textual reconstruction of Hegel's account of tragic heroism as bound up with a developmental theory of moral subjectivity.",
    keywords = "Hegel, Sophocles, Oedipus, tragic-hero, moral-accountability, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: number, year:2018->2020",
    abstract = "Abstract This paper argues that Hegel’s account of subjectivity and agency as historically coined is essential to an accurate understanding of his theory of tragedy. Focusing on Sophocles’ Oedipus the King, I argue that Hegel’s historical account of agency is necessary for understanding his theory of the ancient tragic hero. Although Hegel’s theory of ancient tragedy is often described in terms of a conflict between ethical spheres embodied in two individuals, the conflict in Oedipus is between Oedipus’ deeds and his later knowledge of what has actually occurred. I show how this seemingly subjective conflict is in keeping with Hegel’s theory. Further, while Hegel sees Oedipus as wrong to take full moral accountability for the consequences of his deeds, at the same time, for Hegel, this is the right action for a tragic hero, and the very thing that renders Oedipus timelessly and tragically heroic, rather than a mere victim of fate.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{donougho2006hegels,
    author = "Donougho, Martin",
    title = "Hegel's Pragmatics of Tragedy",
    journal = "Idealistic Studies",
    year = "2006",
    volume = "36",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Donougho argues that the secondary literature has tended to focus on the ethical content of Hegel's tragic theory (the tragic) at the expense of cultic form and dramaturgical enactment (tragedy), and has overlooked the different linguistic levels Hegel employs. He argues the term "Individualität" allows Hegel, in Phenomenology chapter VI, to describe a logic of equivocal representation at work in ancient Sittlichkeit, and that Hegel's conception of tragedy is misrepresented if the claims made about "art-religion" in chapter VII--where tragedy takes on a meta-aesthetic color--are excluded. Hegel sees tragedy as more than an ancient phenomenon: a recurring feature in attempts to represent (vorstellen) a speculative truth in sensuous form. RELEVANCE: Complements the Aesthetics-centered literature by showing how the tragic collision-and-reconciliation structure already operates in the Phenomenology's account of ethical life, which is the text most directly at stake in the project's Antigone-centered domains; helps connect the aesthetic theory of tragedy to the systematic role tragedy plays in Hegel's account of Spirit's self-development. POSITION: A performative/pragmatic reading of Hegel's tragic theory that emphasizes its dramaturgical enactment across the Phenomenology rather than confining it to the Aesthetics lectures' ethical content.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Phenomenology-of-Spirit, tragic-theory, performativity, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: number, pages, doi",
    abstract = "This paper attempts in a preliminary way to bring out the 'pragmatics' or 'performativity' in Hegel's conception of tragedy and the tragic in the Phenom- enology of Spirit. The secondary literature has tended to focus on ethical content (the tragic) at the expense of cultic form and dramaturgical enactment (tragedy); and even with the tragic it has tended to overlook the different linguistic levels in use. I argue that the peculiar term 'Individualitat' allows Hegel, in chapter VI, to describe a logic of equivocal representation he sees at work in ancient 'Sittlichkeit' (ethical life). I argue furthermore that we seriously misrepresent Hegel's concep- tion of tragedy if we do not include the astonishing claims made of 'Art-religion' in chapter VII. Here tragedy takes on a meta-aesthetic color. Hegel sees tragedy as more than an ancient phenomenon, but as a recurring feature in attempts to represent (vorstellen) a speculative truth in sensuous form.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{billings2014genealogy,
    author = "Billings, Joshua",
    title = "Genealogy of the Tragic: Greek Tragedy and German Philosophy",
    year = "2014",
    address = "Princeton, NJ",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Billings traces the emergence of "the tragic" as a distinct philosophical category in Germany around 1800, arguing that post-Kantian writers (Schiller, the young Schelling, the young Hegel, the Schlegel brothers, Hölderlin) turned to ancient Greek tragedy, especially Sophocles' Oedipus Tyrannus and Antigone, because it offered a historicized model of freedom that resonated with debates opened by Kant's third Critique. He devotes his final chapters to Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit and Hölderlin's "Notes" on Oedipus and Antigone as the two most complex post-Kantian treatments, in which tragedy becomes a "figure" for understanding historicity itself. RELEVANCE: Supplies the authoritative intellectual history situating Hegel's theory of tragedy within its immediate German philosophical context, allowing the project to distinguish what is distinctively Hegelian in the treatment of Antigone from what belongs to a broader contemporaneous discourse shared with Schelling and Hölderlin (both of whom also engaged closely with Antigone and Sophoclean tragedy). POSITION: An intellectual-historical account of "the tragic" as a genre of philosophical theorizing distinct from tragedy as dramatic genre, treating Hegel as one figure--alongside Hölderlin--within a wider genealogy rather than as its culmination.},
    keywords = "Hegel, genealogy-of-the-tragic, German-idealism, Schelling, Hoelderlin, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {It is well known that the publication of Kant's Critique of the Power of Judgment in 1790 sparked intense debates among German philosophers and intellectuals, which gave birth to what would later be referred to as German Idealism or post-Kantian idealist philosophy. Those debates, which included authors such as Reinhold, Fichte, Schelling, Schiller, Hölderlin, and Hegel, revolved around the exact significance of Kant's self-proclaimed philosophical revolution. From these debates concerning Kant's philosophical and France's political revolutions, some of the above-mentioned authors were drawn to reexamine Ancient Greece with particular attention to ancient Greek tragedy. Joshua Billings' book explores just this, and offers a remarkably well-documented study of one of the most intriguing trains of thought in the development of late 18th century and early 19th century post-Kantian German philosophy. Billings devotes his final chapters to what he contends are two seminal contributions to the post-Kantian reflection on Greek tragedy: Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) and Hölderlin's Sophocles: Oedipus and Antigone (1804), arguing both take the connection between post-Kantian philosophy and ancient tragedy a step further by treating tragedy as a "model" or "figure" for understanding temporality and historicity.},
    abstract_source = "ndpr"
}

@misc{houlgate1986hegel,
    author = "Houlgate, Stephen",
    title = "Hegel, Nietzsche and the Criticism of Metaphysics",
    year = "1986",
    address = "New York",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Houlgate uses tragedy as the focal theme for a sustained comparison of Hegel and Nietzsche, arguing (in the chapter "Hegel and Nietzsche on Tragedy") that Nietzsche's critique of metaphysics itself relies on a Life/Thought distinction that constitutes a metaphysical dualism of the kind Nietzsche attacks, whereas Hegel's dialectical philosophy--which treats alleged opposites as defining components of a dynamic--offers a more profound critique of metaphysical dualism. The book more broadly evaluates the Hegel-Nietzsche relationship as a debate over the legitimacy of metaphysics itself, comparing Nietzsche's Dionysian philosophy with Hegel's dialectical philosophy by focusing on tragedy as a subject central to both. RELEVANCE: The classic sustained treatment of Hegel versus Nietzsche specifically on tragedy, directly addressing whether Hegel's reconciliatory theory is naively optimistic compared to Nietzsche's affirmation of tragic conflict, or whether Nietzsche's alternative is itself less radical than it claims. POSITION: Defends Hegel's dialectical, reconciliatory theory of tragedy against the Nietzschean critique, arguing Nietzsche remains within the metaphysical horizon he claims to reject.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Nietzsche, tragic-theory, metaphysics-critique, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "This study of Hegel and Nietzsche evaluates and compares their work through their common criticism of the metaphysics for operating with conceptual oppositions such as being/becoming and egoism/altruism. Dr Houlgate exposes Nietzsche's critique as employing the distinction of Life and Thought, which itself constitutes a metaphysical dualism of the kind Nietzsche attacks. By comparison Hegel is shown to provide a more profound critique of metaphysical dualism by applying his philosophy of the dialectic, which sees such alleged opposites as defining components of a dynamic. In choosing to study a theme so fundamental to both philosophers' work, Houlgate has established a framework within which to evaluate the Hegel-Nietzsche debate; to make the first full study of Nietzsche's view of Hegel's work; and to compare Nietzsche's Dionysic philosophy with Hegel's dialectical philosophy by focusing on tragedy, a subject central to the philosophy of both.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{roche1998tragedy,
    author = "Roche, Mark William",
    title = "Tragedy and Comedy: A Systematic Study and a Critique of Hegel",
    year = "1998",
    address = "Albany",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Roche offers what he presents as the first sustained evaluation and critique of Hegel's theory of tragedy and comedy taken together, using that critique as the basis for developing an original, independent systematic theory of both dramatic genres. The book works through Hegel's account of collision, ethical substance, and reconciliation in detail before identifying where Roche thinks the theory requires revision or extension. RELEVANCE: The most comprehensive single study of Hegel's theory of tragedy and comedy as a unified system, providing a detailed map of the general theoretical apparatus (collision, substance, reconciliation) that any close reading of the Antigone chapter must presuppose, along with a critical perspective on the theory's limits. POSITION: A systematic, partly critical reconstruction that treats Hegel's theory as the necessary starting point for, but not the final word on, a philosophy of tragedy and comedy.",
    keywords = "Hegel, tragic-theory, comedy, systematic-study, INCOMPLETE, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{alznauer2021hegel,
    editor = "Alznauer, Mark",
    title = "Hegel on Tragedy and Comedy: New Essays",
    year = "2021",
    address = "Albany",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: This edited collection explores the full extent of Hegel's engagement with tragedy and comedy across his corpus, extending from literary and dramatic-form questions to the role these genres play in Hegel's account of the history of society and religion. Contributions address topics including Greek tragedy as the archetype of tragic drama, the historical reception of Hegel's theory of Greek tragedy, and Hegel's treatment of comedy and religious experience. RELEVANCE: The most recent, comprehensive scholarly collection dedicated specifically to Hegel's theory of tragedy and comedy, useful for identifying the current state of debate and for locating essays that address dramatic-form questions (chorus, genre archetypes) bearing directly on how Hegel's Antigone reading functions as an instance of a general theory of Greek tragedy as "archetype." POSITION: Represents the current, pluralistic state of Anglophone Hegel-and-tragedy scholarship rather than a single interpretive thesis.},
    keywords = "Hegel, tragic-theory, comedy, edited-volume, dramatic-form, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Explores the full extent of Hegel’s interest in tragedy and comedy throughout his works and extends from more literary and dramatic issues to questions about the role these genres play in the history of society and religion.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{schmidt2001germans,
    author = "Schmidt, Dennis J.",
    title = "On Germans and Other Greeks: Tragedy and Ethical Life",
    year = "2001",
    address = "Bloomington",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Schmidt examines tragedy as one of the highest forms of human expression for both the ancients and the moderns, tracing how German philosophy--especially Hegel and Heidegger--turned to Greek tragedy, and to Antigone in particular, to lay out the foundations and complexities of ethical life (Sittlichkeit) in the present historical moment. RELEVANCE: Directly connects the philosophy-of-tragedy literature to the project's core theme of Sittlichkeit, showing that Hegel's turn to Antigone to ground an account of ethical life is itself part of a broader German philosophical strategy (shared with, and later transformed by, Heidegger) of using tragedy to think through the structure and fragility of ethical community. POSITION: A continental, broadly sympathetic account of the German philosophical tradition's use of Greek tragedy to articulate ethical life, treating Hegel and Heidegger as complementary rather than opposed moments in that tradition.",
    keywords = "Hegel, Heidegger, tragedy, ethical-life, Sittlichkeit, Antigone, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "What Greek tragedy and German philosophy reveal about the meaning of art for ethical life.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{galle1993disjunction,
    author = "Galle, Roland",
    editor = "Georgopoulos, N.",
    title = "The Disjunction of the Tragic: Hegel and Nietzsche",
    year = "1993",
    pages = "39--56",
    doi = "10.1007/978-1-349-22759-4\_3",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Galle argues that philosophic speculation on tragedy and the tragic did not develop until German idealism, at precisely the moment when tragedy as a dramatic practice had entered a far-reaching structural crisis often diagnosed as the "death of tragedy" in modern times; he invokes Hegel's own figure of Minerva's owl (which begins its flight only at nightfall) to characterize this relationship between the historical decline of tragic practice and the rise of philosophical theorizing about it. Against this shared backdrop, he traces the disjunction between Hegel's dialectical model of conflict resolved through reconciliation and Nietzsche's affirmation of unresolved Dionysian conflict as constitutive of tragic art and existence. RELEVANCE: A focused contribution to the specific comparative question animating this domain--whether Hegel's theory is optimistic and reconciliatory relative to Nietzsche--offered as one chapter within a broader edited collection surveying the philosophy of tragedy from antiquity onward. POSITION: Frames Hegel and Nietzsche as representing a fundamental "disjunction" within the philosophy of the tragic rather than a simple succession, positioning Hegel on the reconciliatory side of the divide.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Nietzsche, tragic-theory, disjunction, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: booktitle, publisher, type:@incollection->@misc",
    abstract = "Philosophic speculation on tragedy and the tragic did not develop until the period of German idealism, thus at a time in which tragedy itself had gotten into a far-reaching structural crisis. This crisis has served many critics as an indicator of the frequently diagnosed 'Death of Tragedy' in modern times. Thus we have to proceed from the remarkable phenomenon that large scale philosophic speculation on the tragic begins at a time in which the secular tradition of tragedy itself seems to have reached its end. Hegel's figure of speech of Minerva's owl, which doesn't begin its flight until nightfall, has been cited to comment on this striking relationship between the crisis of poetic practice and the onset of philosophic speculation relating to this practice.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{beaney2009beautiful,
    author = "Beaney, Tara",
    title = "Beautiful Death: The Nineteenth-Century Fascination with Antigone",
    year = "2009",
    doi = "10.5334/opt.070902",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Beaney investigates the reception of Sophocles' Antigone in early nineteenth-century Germany, focusing on Hegel and Hölderlin as the two most influential thinkers of the period to engage the play. She takes a sociohistorical approach to the long-standing philosophical problem of how tragic representations of suffering and death--above all Antigone's self-sacrifice--can arouse aesthetic pleasure and fascination, asking whether the era's fascination with the play was in part a fascination with the topos of female death. RELEVANCE: Situates Hegel's reading of Antigone within the broader nineteenth-century German reception of the play (alongside Hölderlin), and raises the "paradox of tragedy" (pleasure in tragic suffering) specifically in relation to Antigone's death and its gendered framing, connecting the aesthetic-theoretical domain to reception-historical questions. POSITION: A reception-historical account that treats the Hegel/ Hölderlin fascination with Antigone as inseparable from broader cultural preoccupations with beautiful, feminized death, rather than as purely philosophical argument.},
    keywords = "Hegel, Hoelderlin, Antigone, reception-history, paradox-of-tragedy, Low, METADATA\_CLEANED: journal, number, type:@article->@misc",
    abstract = "This article investigates the reception of Sophocles’ Antigone in early nineteenth-century Germany, a period in which interest in ancient Greek tragedy flourished, with Antigone being particularly prominent. The essay considers two influential figures of the period who regarded the work highly: the philosopher G.W.F. Hegel (1770-1831) and the poet Friedrich Holderlin (1770- 1843). In discussing their attitudes towards Antigone, the article raises the long-standing philosophical problem of how tragic representations of suffering and death, such as Antigone’s self-sacrifice, can arouse aesthetic pleasure and fascination. To address this problem, a sociohistorical approach is taken, exploring how the topos of female death may have appealed to the imagination of these important thinkers.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@misc{leonard2005athens,
    author = "Leonard, Miriam",
    title = "Athens in Paris: Ancient Greece and the Political in Post-War French Thought",
    year = "2005",
    address = "Oxford",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Leonard investigates how post-war France turned to ancient Greece--Plato, Sophocles' Oedipus Tyrannus, and the ancient polis among the recurring reference points--to formulate a new interrogation of the political. She traces how a group of influential Parisian thinkers (Lacan, Derrida, Foucault, Irigaray, Vernant) drew on ancient Greek material to debate political subjectivity and ethical choice, arguing that ancient Greek writing played a decisive, and under-acknowledged, part in shaping the intellectual projects of structuralism and post-structuralism. RELEVANCE: Extends the "genealogy of the tragic" traced by Billings into its twentieth-century French afterlife, showing how the German-idealist habit of reading Greek tragedy and Greek antiquity as a resource for thinking the political persisted--transformed through psychoanalysis and structuralism--well beyond Hegel; useful for situating Hegel's Antigone within the longer chain of philosophical appropriations of Greek tragedy relevant to the project's comparative domains, though the book's own focus is French reception rather than Hegel's theory as such. POSITION: A reception-historical study of the political uses of ancient Greece in post-war French thought, positioned downstream of (and largely outside the direct scope of) the German idealist tradition addressed elsewhere in this domain.},
    keywords = "Hegel, reception-history, French-thought, Greek-tragedy, Low, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, doi, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Abstract Why was Derrida reading Plato as students stormed the Sorbonne in May '68? Why was Foucault writing about Sophocles' Oedipus Tyrannus while fighting for the rights of prisoners with Vidal-Naquet? What has Vernant the resistance fighter to do with Vernant theorist of the ancient polis? This book investigates how post-war France turned to ancient Greece to formulate a new interrogation of the political. It investigates why a group of highly influential Parisian thinkers rediscovered ancient Athens to debate the role of political subjectivity and ethical choice in the life of the modern citizen. The book explores the ways in which the writings of the ancient Greeks played a decisive part in shaping the intellectual projects of structuralism and post-structuralism — arguably the most significant currents of thought of the post-war era. The authors investigated, including Lacan, Derrida, Foucault, Irigaray, and Vernant, have had an incalculable influence on the direction of classical studies over the last thirty years, but classicists have yet to give due attention to the crucial role of the ancient world in the development of their philosophy.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{hardimon1994social,
    author = {Hardimon, Michael O.},
    title = {Hegel's Social Philosophy: The Project of Reconciliation},
    publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
    year = {1994},
    address = {Cambridge},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hardimon reconstructs Hegel's Philosophy of Right as fundamentally a project of "reconciliation" (Versoehnung), aiming to show individuals that the basic institutions of the modern social world -- family, civil society, and the state -- are rational and hence deserve their allegiance, thereby overcoming the modern experience of alienation from social life. He argues reconciliation requires showing both that modern institutions actually embody freedom and that individuals can recognize this rationality reflectively rather than merely through unreflective custom, distinguishing Hegel's project from ancient Sittlichkeit's immediate identification with one's social role. RELEVANCE: Provides the systematic (Philosophy of Right) counterpart to the Phenomenology's historical narrative of Sittlichkeit collapsing into legal personhood, explaining why Hegel thinks modern subjects need something like "reconciliation" that ancient Greek citizens, on this reading, did not require, since they were unreflectively at home in their ethical world. POSITION: Systematic commentary situating the family/civil-society/state triad within the argumentative aim of reconciling modern individuals to modern institutions, glossed as Hegel's answer to a problem of alienation that immediate ancient ethical life did not yet pose.},
    keywords = {Hegel, Sittlichkeit, reconciliation, Philosophy-of-Right, civil-society, Hardimon, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{smith1989critique,
    author = {Smith, Steven B.},
    title = {Hegel's Critique of Liberalism: Rights in Context},
    publisher = {University of Chicago Press},
    year = {1989},
    address = {Chicago},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Smith argues that Hegel's political philosophy should be read as an internal critique and reformulation of liberal rights-theory rather than its rejection, one that seeks to combine the ancient emphasis on the dignity and quasi-architectonic status of political life with the modern liberal concern for individual freedom, rights, and mutual recognition. He traces this synthesis through Hegel's critique of natural-rights theories and abstract right toward what Smith calls the "Hegelian Rechtsstaat," in which subjective right is preserved but embedded within, and subordinated to, the substantive ethical community of civil society and the state. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the ancient/modern evaluative question at the heart of this domain -- whether Hegel's incorporation of individual right and civil society into ethical life represents a genuine advance beyond Greek Sittlichkeit or an uneasy compromise -- by reading Hegel as attempting to preserve the strengths of both the ancient polis and modern liberalism. POSITION: A liberal-sympathetic reading that treats Hegel as an immanent critic of, rather than an enemy to, liberal rights-based politics.},
    keywords = {Hegel, liberalism, rights, ancient-modern, Philosophy-of-Right, Smith, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{mccarney2000history,
    author = {McCarney, Joseph},
    title = {Hegel on History},
    publisher = {Routledge},
    year = {2000},
    address = {London},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: McCarney offers a systematic introduction to and defense of Hegel's philosophy of history as presented in the Introduction to the Lectures on the Philosophy of World History, reconstructing the core concepts -- reason in history, the cunning of reason, the march of Spirit through successive "world-historical" peoples from the Oriental to the Greek, Roman, and Germanic worlds -- and situating this narrative within Hegel's wider philosophical system. He defends the philosophy of history against charges that it is merely speculative myth-making, arguing it can be read as making a defensible claim about the historical development of the concept of freedom, while also engaging the standard criticisms (determinism, theodicy, Eurocentrism) leveled against it. RELEVANCE: Supplies the authoritative overview of the narrative structure -- Oriental despotism, Greek immediate freedom, Roman abstract right, Germanic/Christian subjective freedom -- within which Hegel situates the polis-to-empire transition discussed in this domain, and directly engages the methodological controversies (teleology, historical accuracy) that this domain's fourth key question raises. POSITION: A sympathetic, reconstructive commentary treating Hegel's philosophy of history as still philosophically defensible once properly distinguished from crude determinism or straightforward empirical history.},
    keywords = {Hegel, philosophy-of-history, teleology, Roman-world, Greek-world, McCarney, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{mccarney2003response,
    author = {McCarney, Joseph},
    title = {Hegel's Racism? A Response to Bernasconi},
    journal = {Radical Philosophy},
    year = {2003},
    volume = {119},
    pages = {32--35},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: McCarney responds to Bernasconi's charge that Hegel's account of world history is organized around racial hierarchies, arguing that Bernasconi overreaches in extending his critique of Enlightenment race-thinking to Hegel specifically, and that the geographical and climatic determinism structuring Hegel's account of "world-historical peoples" should be distinguished from a properly racial doctrine; he contends Hegel's ranking of peoples turns on the development of the concept of freedom within a people's institutions rather than on biological race as such. RELEVANCE: Represents one side of a direct methodological exchange with Bernasconi that bears squarely on this domain's fourth key question -- how commentators debate the teleology, Eurocentrism, and historical accuracy of the philosophy-of-history narrative that carries Sittlichkeit from the Greek world through Rome to the modern Germanic world. POSITION: A defense of Hegel against the charge of racism properly understood, conceding Eurocentric teleology while resisting the stronger charge of race-based hierarchy.},
    keywords = {Hegel, Eurocentrism, race, philosophy-of-history, Bernasconi-exchange, McCarney, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{bernasconi2000philosophy,
    author = {Bernasconi, Robert},
    title = {With What Must the Philosophy of World History Begin? On the Racial Basis of Hegel's Eurocentrism},
    journal = {Nineteenth-Century Contexts},
    year = {2000},
    volume = {22},
    number = {2},
    pages = {171--201},
    doi = {10.1080/08905490008583507},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Bernasconi argues that Hegel's decision about where to "begin" the philosophy of world history -- his exclusion of Africa (understood as sub-Saharan Africa) from world history proper and his treatment of geography and climate as preconditions of a people's capacity for freedom -- reveals a racialized substructure beneath Hegel's ostensibly conceptual narrative of Spirit's development from the Oriental world through Greece and Rome to the Germanic world. He contends this racial basis is not incidental but structurally required by Hegel's need to justify beginning the story of freedom with certain peoples and not others. RELEVANCE: Provides the central methodological critique of the philosophy-of-history narrative framing the Greek-to-Roman-to-modern transition discussed in this domain, directly raising the Eurocentrism and historical-accuracy questions the domain's fourth key question asks commentators to adjudicate. POSITION: A postcolonial and critical-race critique arguing that Hegel's world-historical narrative is racially structured at its foundations, not merely biased in its details.},
    abstract = {(2000). With what must the philosophy of world history begin? On the racial basis of Hegel's eurocentrism. Nineteenth-Century Contexts: Vol. 22, Race and social theory, pp. 171-201.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
    keywords = {Hegel, Eurocentrism, race, philosophy-of-history, Bernasconi-exchange, Bernasconi, High}
}

@book{losurdo2004hegel,
    author = {Losurdo, Domenico},
    title = {Hegel and the Freedom of Moderns},
    publisher = {Duke University Press},
    year = {2004},
    address = {Durham, NC},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Losurdo argues against liberal and left-liberal readings that treat Hegel as either an apologist for Prussian reaction or a proto-totalitarian, contending instead that Hegel's defense of the modern state -- including its interventions to ameliorate poverty and its incorporation of civil society and individual right -- represents a distinctively engaged, historically grounded defense of modern freedom, continuous with concrete political struggles of Hegel's own time rather than an abstract system detached from them. He reconstructs Hegel's relationship to the French Revolution, the Prussian reform movement, and contemporary rights debates to argue that "the freedom of moderns" in Hegel is a substantive, institutionally embedded achievement rather than either empty liberal proceduralism or an authoritarian negation of individual freedom. RELEVANCE: Offers a strong "gain" reading of the ancient/modern Sittlichkeit contrast -- treating Hegel's modern state, with its differentiated civil society and legally guaranteed rights, as an advance in the concrete realization of freedom beyond both ancient immediacy and liberal abstraction -- directly bearing on this domain's third key question about whether modernity represents a loss, gain, or ambivalent development relative to Greek Sittlichkeit. POSITION: A historically contextualized, left-leaning defense of Hegel's modern state against both liberal and totalitarian-reading critics.},
    keywords = {Hegel, modernity, freedom, civil-society, ancient-modern, Losurdo, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{loickkautzer2015expression,
    author = {Loick, Daniel and Kautzer, Chad},
    title = {'Expression of Contempt': Hegel's Critique of Legal Freedom},
    journal = {Law and Critique},
    year = {2015},
    volume = {26},
    number = {2},
    pages = {189--206},
    doi = {10.1007/s10978-015-9159-7},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Loick reconstructs Hegel's critique of the "subjectivity of right" via the short "Condition of Right" (Rechtszustand) chapter of the Phenomenology of Spirit, in which the collapse of Greek ethical substance into the Roman legal order reduces the individual to an abstract legal "person" whose entire social standing is guaranteed externally by positive law rather than lived through concrete ethical relationships; he argues Hegel's historical analysis (drawing also on the Lectures on the Philosophy of History) diagnoses this legal abstraction as a form of alienation -- an "expression of contempt" for concrete individuality -- even as Hegel ultimately locates the remedy not in abolishing abstract right but in embedding it within richer ethical spheres. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses this domain's first key question by offering a close reading of precisely the Phenomenology passage in which Hegel narrates the shift from immediate Greek Sittlichkeit to abstract Roman legal personhood, and assesses what philosophical work this diagnosis does and whether Hegel's own proposed remedy is adequate to the problem he identifies. POSITION: An immanent-critical reading sympathetic to Hegel's diagnosis of legal abstraction as alienating, but skeptical that his prescribed institutional remedy (embedding right within ethical life) actually resolves the problem.},
    keywords = {Hegel, Rechtszustand, legal-personhood, Roman-world, Phenomenology, alienation, Loick, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{hristov2022roman,
    author = {Hristov, George},
    title = {Roman Courage and Constitution in Hegel's Philosophy of Right},
    journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
    year = {2022},
    volume = {43},
    number = {2},
    pages = {242--266},
    doi = {10.1017/hgl.2022.2},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hristov argues that Hegel's account of the modern constitutional state, though intended to overcome the fragility of merely legal or formal citizenship, in fact reproduces structural features of the Roman republican model it is meant to supersede: because Hegel's citizens attain political freedom only through mutual recognition secured by institutions rather than through a stable, self-sustaining ethical substance, they remain vulnerable to a cyclical process of mutual enslavement and dissolution analogous to Rome's republican decline, a fragility Hegel's account of civic "courage" is meant to counteract but cannot fully resolve. RELEVANCE: Uses the explicit Roman republican background of Hegel's constitutional theory to probe whether the transition from Greek Sittlichkeit through Roman legal status genuinely gets superseded in the modern rational state, or whether Roman legal fragility persists as an unresolved problem within Hegel's systematic account of the state in the Philosophy of Right. POSITION: A critical reading that finds an unresolved Roman residue -- structural fragility of legally mediated freedom -- within Hegel's ostensibly superior modern constitutional state.},
    abstract = {Abstract This article argues that the citizens of Hegel's state cannot maintain themselves as politically free because they are susceptible to mutual enslavement. I demonstrate this by focusing on the Roman republican background of Hegel's constitution, the potential trajectory of its dissolution and the accompanying means of its cyclical fortification through courage. Hegel, by integrating aspects of Roman republicanism into his account of the modern constitutional state, imports along with it a structural vulnerability to the dissolution he means to overcome.},
    abstract_source = {s2},
    keywords = {Hegel, Rome, courage, constitution, state, Philosophy-of-Right, Hristov, Medium}
}

@book{tibebu2011hegel,
    author = {Tibebu, Teshale},
    title = {Hegel and the Third World: The Making of Eurocentrism in World History},
    publisher = {Syracuse University Press},
    year = {2011},
    address = {Syracuse, NY},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Tibebu offers a comprehensive critique of Hegel's philosophy of history, arguing that Hegel's narrative -- which moves from the "Oriental" world through Greece and Rome to the modern Germanic-Christian world -- systematically constructs Europe as the culmination of world history while relegating Africa, and much of Asia, to a prehistorical or static status outside genuine historical development; he situates this Eurocentrism within Hegel's broader dialectical method, arguing it is not merely an unfortunate empirical error but built into the logical structure of Hegel's teleological conception of Spirit's self-realization. RELEVANCE: Provides an extended, book-length treatment of the historical-accuracy and Eurocentrism debate raised by this domain's fourth key question, situating the specific Greek-to-Roman transition within the larger world-historical scheme (Oriental-Greek-Roman-Germanic) that gives that transition its philosophical significance for Hegel. POSITION: A sustained postcolonial critique arguing that Eurocentrism is constitutive of, rather than incidental to, Hegel's dialectical philosophy of history.},
    keywords = {Hegel, Eurocentrism, philosophy-of-history, colonialism, postcolonial, Tibebu, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{bykova2020bildung,
    author = {Bykova, Marina F.},
    title = {Hegel's Philosophy of Bildung},
    booktitle = {The Palgrave Hegel Handbook},
    editor = {Bykova, Marina F. and Westphal, Kenneth R.},
    publisher = {Palgrave Macmillan},
    year = {2020},
    address = {Cham},
    pages = {425--449},
    doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-26597-7_22},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Bykova examines Hegel's concept of Bildung -- cultivation, formation, or self-formation -- tracing its systematic role across Hegel's philosophy, including its function in the Phenomenology of Spirit's account of the "world of self-alienated spirit" (the world of culture that succeeds ethical substance) and its function in the Philosophy of Right's account of civil society, where Bildung names the process by which particular individuals are educated into universal, rationally mediated selfhood. RELEVANCE: Addresses the "world of culture" (Bildung) moment named explicitly in this domain's focus, connecting the concept's historical appearance in the Phenomenology's post-Sittlichkeit narrative to its systematic function in civil society's formation of modern individuals within the Philosophy of Right. POSITION: An expository systematic account of Bildung as a unifying concept linking Hegel's historical narrative of spirit's self-alienation to his systematic theory of modern ethical life.},
    keywords = {Hegel, Bildung, culture, self-alienated-spirit, civil-society, Bykova, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{wolsing2022hegel,
    author = {Wolsing, Peter},
    title = {Hegel on Nation, Ethical Life, and the Modern State},
    journal = {Danish Yearbook of Philosophy},
    year = {2022},
    volume = {55},
    number = {2},
    pages = {199--218},
    doi = {10.1163/24689300-bja10028},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Wolsing examines the role of "nation" in Hegel's theory of the modern state, arguing that Hegel strikes a balance between historicism and a rational justification of state and law by grounding the state in a notion of Sittlichkeit that is both historically and nationally particular and subject to a world-historical development toward rationality and universal right; he contends Hegel's "ethical life" is national in the sense of being a historically changeable community of values sustained by citizens' conscious participation (patriotism), not a matter of primordial ethnic or linguistic identity, which distinguishes Hegel's modern nation-state from later nationalist invocations of primordial culture. RELEVANCE: Bears on this domain's third key question by clarifying in what sense modern Sittlichkeit, for Hegel, remains particular and historically situated (like Greek Sittlichkeit) even as it incorporates universal subjective freedom and right, suggesting the ancient/modern relationship is one of formal continuity (particular ethical substance) combined with substantive transformation (incorporation of universal right). POSITION: A systematic reading that resists both purely universalist and straightforwardly nationalist readings of Hegel's modern ethical life.},
    abstract = {This paper examines Hegel's idea of nation and its significance for his theory of the modern state, namely, the role that 'the national' plays for his justification of right in the Philosophy of Right. It is argued that Hegel strikes a balance between historicism and a rational justification of state and law. He bases the state on a notion of Sittlichkeit (ethical life) that is both national and subjected to a world historical development toward rationality and universal right. Consequently, 'nation,' in the sense of a group of people invoking identity and rights based on a primordial common language, culture, and territory, does not cover what Hegel means by the modern nation state. 'Ethical life' is national, but it also constitutes a historically changeable community of values supported by citizens' conscious participation in communal life (patriotism). Today, Hegel's idea undermines the legitimacy of nationalistic invocations of primordial ethnic cultures within politics.},
    abstract_source = {s2},
    keywords = {Hegel, nation, ethical-life, modern-state, patriotism, Wolsing, Medium}
}

@article{houlgate1997hegels,
    author = {Houlgate, Stephen},
    title = {Hegel's Critique of the Triumph of Verstand in Modernity},
    journal = {Bulletin of the Hegel Society of Great Britain},
    year = {1997},
    volume = {18},
    number = {1},
    pages = {54--70},
    doi = {10.1017/s0263523200001191},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Houlgate argues that Hegel's philosophy of history diagnoses the French Revolution's legacy -- the principle that "the subjective wills of the many should hold sway," which Hegel associates with liberalism and majority rule -- as installing merely formal, abstract understanding (Verstand) rather than genuine reason (Vernunft) at the heart of the modern state; he contends Hegel judges this triumph of Verstand to have distorted rather than perfected modern political rationality, because it fails to grasp the state as an organic, mediated whole in the way ancient Sittlichkeit, for all its immediacy, at least implicitly did. RELEVANCE: Directly engages this domain's third key question by showing that Hegel's assessment of modern subjectivity and individual right is not straightforwardly progressivist: modern liberal principles, on Hegel's own diagnosis, can distort rather than complete the rational state, complicating any simple "gain" narrative relative to Greek Sittlichkeit. POSITION: A critical reading emphasizing Hegel's ambivalence toward modern liberal subjectivity, reading the "triumph of Verstand" as a genuine loss internal to modernity's own self-understanding.},
    abstract = {In his lectures on the philosophy of history Hegel passes this famous judgement on the French Revolution. "Anaxagoras had been the first to say that nous governs the world; but only now did humanity come to recognize that thought should rule spiritual actuality. This was thus a magnificent dawn". What first gave rise to discontent in France, in Hegel's view, were the heavy burdens that pressed upon the people and the government's inability to procure for the Court the means of supporting its luxury and extravagance. But soon the new spirit of freedom and enlightenment began to stir in men's minds and carry them forward to revolution. Hegel points out that the legacy of the revolution is actually an ambiguous one. For, although the principles which guided the revolution were those of reason -- namely, that humanity is born to freedom and self-determination -- they were held fast in their abstraction and turned polemically, and at times terribly, against the existing order. What ultimately triumphed in the revolution was thus not concrete reason itself, but abstract reason or understanding. In Hegel's view, the enduring legacy of such revolutionary understanding was the principle that "the subjective wills of the many should hold sway," the principle of "liberalism" or majority rule, which has since spread from France to become one of the governing principles of the modern state. What is of crucial importance for Hegel, however, is that such measures have not rendered the state more modern and rational, but have in fact distorted the modern state.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
    keywords = {Hegel, Verstand, modernity, liberalism, French-Revolution, ancient-modern, Houlgate, Medium}
}

@article{baumann2020was,
    author = {Baumann, Charlotte},
    title = {Was Hegel an Authoritarian Thinker? Reading Hegel's Philosophy of History on the Basis of His Metaphysics},
    journal = {Archiv für Geschichte der Philosophie},
    year = {2020},
    volume = {103},
    number = {1},
    pages = {120--147},
    doi = {10.1515/agph-2017-0128},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Baumann addresses the long-standing charge -- from Marx to Popper and Habermas -- that Hegel's metaphysics of history has anti-individualist and authoritarian implications, because Hegel identifies historical progress with reason imposing itself on individuals via the "cunning of reason" and treats world-historical individuals as mere instruments of Spirit; she argues that a proper understanding of Hegel's metaphysics shows historical progress and individual freedom to be mutually constitutive rather than opposed, defusing the authoritarian reading. RELEVANCE: Engages directly with this domain's fourth key question regarding the methodological and political stakes of Hegel's philosophy-of-history narrative, testing whether the teleological structure that carries Sittlichkeit from the Greek world through Rome to the modern state licenses an authoritarian subordination of individuals to historical necessity. POSITION: A metaphysically grounded defense of Hegel against the authoritarian-historicist reading associated with Popper and the early Frankfurt School critique.},
    abstract = {Abstract With Hegel's metaphysics attracting renewed attention, it is time to address a long-standing criticism: Scholars from Marx to Popper and Habermas have worried that Hegel's metaphysics has anti-individualist and authoritarian implications, which are particularly pronounced in his Philosophy of History, since Hegel identifies historical progress with reason imposing itself on individuals.},
    abstract_source = {s2},
    keywords = {Hegel, authoritarianism, philosophy-of-history, metaphysics, Popper, Baumann, Medium}
}

@article{lima2024hegel,
    author = {Lima, Erick},
    title = {Hegel contra Hegel: Eurocentrism, Colonialism, and Progress},
    journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
    year = {2024},
    volume = {45},
    number = {2},
    pages = {237--264},
    doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.8},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Lima investigates whether Hegel's own philosophical resources can be turned against the Eurocentric and colonialist elements of his thought, arguing that the connection Hegel draws between "spirit" and "progress" can be reconstructed, following Adorno's critique, to recover an experiential dimension of the "progress in the consciousness of freedom" capable of registering its own regressive moments -- including colonial violence -- rather than simply legitimating them. RELEVANCE: Offers a recent contribution to the methodological debate raised by this domain's fourth key question, testing whether Hegel's teleological philosophy of history is irredeemably Eurocentric or whether immanent critique (using Hegel's own concepts against his explicit historical judgments) can separate a philosophically defensible core from the objectionable colonialist content. POSITION: An immanent-critical, Adorno-inflected reading that seeks to rescue elements of Hegel's philosophy of progress from his own Eurocentric and colonialist judgments.},
    abstract = {Abstract This study aims to investigate whether some of the Eurocentric and colonialist contents of Hegel's thought are open to criticism with elements of his own philosophy. First, I intend to show that some of these contents can be organized around the connection between 'spirit' and 'progress'. I then construct an interpretation of Hegel's notion of spirit, based upon which I discuss its possibly pro-colonialist tendencies, arguing that disconnected from the philosophy of history it establishes a connection of autonomy and critique crucial even for anti-colonialist thought. Furthermore, following Adorno's criticism of Hegel, I investigate the possibility of finding in the 'progress in the consciousness of freedom' an experiential dimension capable of capturing its regressive moments.},
    abstract_source = {s2},
    keywords = {Hegel, Eurocentrism, colonialism, progress, Adorno, immanent-critique, Lima, Medium}
}

@book{vernant1988myth,
    author = {Vernant, Jean-Pierre and Vidal-Naquet, Pierre},
    title = {Myth and Tragedy in Ancient Greece},
    year = {1988},
    publisher = {Zone Books},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Vernant and Vidal-Naquet argue that fifth-century Athenian tragedy stages a genuine and historically specific tension between an emergent legal-political vocabulary of decision, responsibility, and citizenship and older religious, mythic, and kinship-based modes of thought that this new vocabulary could not fully absorb. On their structuralist-anthropological method, tragic "ambiguity" is not a stage on the way to conceptual synthesis but a permanent structural feature of a society caught between two systems of value. RELEVANCE: This book is the founding statement of the classicist tradition that treats Sophoclean conflict as historically located and irresolvable rather than as raw material for a dialectical Aufhebung, providing the methodological counterweight against which Hegel's confident resolution of divine law and human law into a higher unity can be tested for historical adequacy. POSITION: Structuralist/anthropological history of religion and law, foundational for later classicist critiques (Sourvinou-Inwood, Oudemans and Lardinois) of philosophically over-determined readings of Greek tragedy.},
    keywords = {Vernant, Vidal-Naquet, structuralism, Greek religion and law, tragic ambiguity, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{nussbaum2001antigone,
    author = {Nussbaum, Martha C.},
    title = {Sophocles' {Antigone}: Conflict, Vision, and Simplification},
    booktitle = {The Fragility of Goodness: Luck and Ethics in Greek Tragedy and Philosophy},
    year = {2001},
    pages = {51--82},
    publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
    doi = {10.1017/CBO9780511817915.006},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Nussbaum reads the Antigone not as a clean opposition between two justified ethical standpoints (family/religion vs. state) but as a play about two characters, Antigone and Creon, who each simplify a genuinely plural ethical world by suppressing values that ought to have claims on them; the tragedy exposes the cost of this simplification rather than vindicating either side, and shows how far both protagonists' fates depend on factors — moral luck — beyond their control. RELEVANCE: Directly contests the Hegelian premise that Antigone and Creon each perfectly and one-sidedly embody a substantial ethical power whose collision is necessary; Nussbaum's emphasis on simplification and moral luck offers a rival diagnosis of the play's catastrophe that does not require, and implicitly rejects, Hegel's schema of two equally "right" and equally "guilty" ethical substances. POSITION: Philosophical (Aristotelian/particularist) reading of Greek tragic ethics, emphasizing moral luck and value pluralism against both Kantian and Hegelian systematizations of tragic conflict.},
    keywords = {Nussbaum, moral luck, Antigone, ethical simplification, tragic conflict, High},
    abstract = {Agamemnon and Eteocles found themselves, through no fault of their own, in situations where revulsion, remorse, and painful memory seemed, for the person of good character, not only inevitable, but also appropriate. This fact, however, someone might concede while still insisting that it is the part of practical wisdom to avoid such situations as far as possible in planning a life. Agamemnon's was an extreme and unpredictable catastrophe. If human beings cannot make themselves entirely safe against such rare bad luck, at least they can structure their lives and commitments so that in the ordinary course of events they will be able to stay clear of serious conflict. One obvious way to do this is to simplify the structure of one's value-commitments, refusing to attach oneself to concerns that frequently, or even infrequently, generate conflicting demands. The avoidance of practical conflict at this level has frequently been thought to be a criterion of rationality for persons — just as it has frequently been thought to be a condition of rationality for a political system that it should order things so that the sincere efforts of such persons will regularly meet with success. This view was known in fifth-century Athens. It is a prominent theme of tragedy: for the painful experiences recorded in our last chapter naturally prompt questions about their own elimination. And it has become firmly entrenched in modern thought, pressed even by some who defend a 'tragic view' of individual cases of practical conflict.},
    abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@book{williams1993shame,
    author = {Williams, Bernard},
    title = {Shame and Necessity},
    year = {1993},
    publisher = {University of California Press},
    doi = {10.1525/9780520915282},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Williams argues against the standard "progressivist" narrative — of which Hegel's account of the movement from Greek ethical substance to modern subjective freedom is a paradigm instance — according to which the Greeks possessed only a primitive "shame culture" governed by external necessity, later superseded by a modern "guilt culture" of internalized, reflective moral agency. Drawing on Homer and tragedy (including close readings of responsibility in Sophocles), Williams contends that the Greeks already had a sophisticated conception of agency, responsibility, and practical necessity, and that the moral psychology we take to be distinctively modern is largely continuous with, rather than an advance upon, the Greek outlook. RELEVANCE: Directly undermines the developmental logic that licenses Hegel's placement of Sittlichkeit at an immature, "beautiful" but not-yet-free stage of Spirit superseded by modern moral subjectivity; if Williams is right that the Greek/modern contrast is far less sharp than the progress narrative requires, Hegel's reading of Antigone as exhibiting an ethical life not yet capable of reflective, individuated freedom is put in serious question. POSITION: Anti-progressivist moral philosophy informed by classical philology; a central philosophical counter-authority to any Hegelian (or more broadly teleological) history of ethical consciousness.},
    keywords = {Williams, shame and guilt, necessity, moral psychology, anti-progressivism, High},
    abstract = {We tend to suppose that the ancient Greeks had primitive ideas of the self, of responsibility, freedom, and shame, and that now humanity has advanced from these to a more refined moral consciousness. Bernard Williams's original and radical book questions this picture of Western history. While we are in many ways different from the Greeks, Williams claims that the differences are not to be traced to a shift in these basic conceptions of ethical life. We are more like the ancients than we are prepared to acknowledge, and only when this is understood can we properly grasp our most important differences from them, such as our rejection of slavery. The author is a philosopher, but much of his book is directed to writers such as Homer and the tragedians, whom he discusses as poets and not just as materials for philosophy. At the center of his study is the question of how we can understand Greek tragedy at all, when its world is so far from ours. Williams explains how it is that when the ancients speak, they do not merely tell us about themselves, but about ourselves. In a new foreword A. A. Long explores the impact of this volume in the context of Williams's career.},
    abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@article{irwin1994critical,
    author = {Irwin, T. H.},
    title = {Critical Notice of Bernard {Williams}, Shame and Necessity},
    journal = {Apeiron},
    year = {1994},
    volume = {27},
    number = {1},
    doi = {10.1515/apeiron.1994.27.1.45},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Irwin subjects Williams's central claims — that the Greeks already possessed a robust notion of agency and responsibility, and that the shame/guilt distinction cannot bear the historical weight the progress narrative places on it — to sustained philosophical and philological scrutiny, questioning whether Williams's evidence from Homer and tragedy fully supports his anti-progressivist conclusions. RELEVANCE: Provides a philosophically trained classicist's assessment of the very anti-progressivist argument (Williams) that this domain uses to challenge Hegel's developmental story, allowing the review to present the necessity/shame/guilt debate as contested rather than settled. POSITION: Sympathetic but critical engagement with Williams from within ancient philosophy, useful for calibrating how much weight the anti-Hegelian, anti-progressivist reading of Greek ethics can actually bear.},
    keywords = {Irwin, Williams, shame and necessity, moral psychology, review, Medium},
    abstract = {Critical review of B. Williams's book Shame and Necessity (1993), based on lecture courses on classical philosophy and literature given at Berkeley.},
    abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@article{gay1989bernard,
    author = {Gay, Robert J.},
    title = {Bernard {Williams} on Practical Necessity},
    journal = {Mind},
    year = {1989},
    volume = {98},
    number = {392},
    pages = {551--569},
    doi = {10.1093/mind/xcviii.392.551},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Gay examines and criticizes Williams's concept of "practical necessity" — the sense in which an agent can find that only one course of action is really open to them, so that alternatives are not live options despite being logically possible — arguing that Williams's account leaves the relation between practical necessity and moral responsibility underspecified. RELEVANCE: Practical necessity is exactly the category Hegel needs, and needs to locate historically, when he describes Antigone and Creon as unable to do otherwise than act on their one-sided ethical commitments; Gay's philosophical dissection of what "necessity" can and cannot justify in action-assessment supplies independent conceptual resources for evaluating whether Hegel's talk of the "necessity" of tragic guilt is doing legitimate philosophical work or merely asserting what needs to be shown. POSITION: Analytic moral philosophy internal to the Williams debate, useful as a conceptual check on any invocation (Hegelian or otherwise) of "necessity" to ground ethical responsibility in tragic action.},
    keywords = {Gay, Williams, practical necessity, responsibility, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{russell2022free,
    author = {Russell, Paul},
    title = {Free Will and the Tragic Predicament: Making Sense of {Williams}},
    booktitle = {Morality and Agency},
    year = {2022},
    pages = {161--181},
    publisher = {Oxford University Press},
    doi = {10.1093/oso/9780197626566.003.0008},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Russell reconstructs Williams's account of the "tragic predicament" — situations in which an agent is morally implicated in outcomes beyond full voluntary control — as a distinctive contribution to the free will and moral responsibility literature, arguing that tragic cases resist both libertarian and standard compatibilist treatments of control and desert. RELEVANCE: Offers a recent, systematic philosophical framework for exactly the kind of case Hegel treats under the heading of tragic ethical guilt (Antigone, Oedipus); using Russell's "tragic predicament" alongside Hegel's account lets the review assess whether Hegel's notion of a guilt that is "necessary" and yet still guilt is philosophically coherent by the lights of contemporary moral responsibility theory. POSITION: Contemporary analytic philosophy of free will and moral responsibility, extending Williams's legacy; represents the philosophical afterlife of the moral-luck/necessity debate independent of Hegel scholarship.},
    keywords = {Russell, Williams, tragic predicament, free will, moral responsibility, Medium},
    abstract = {This chapter presents an interpretation of Bernard Williams's significant and substantial contributions on the topic of free will and moral responsibility. Williams's fundamental objective, it is argued, is to vindicate moral responsibility by way of freeing it from distortions and misrepresentations imposed on it by "the morality system." Although his earlier work is primarily concerned with the critique of "morality" and its associated understanding of responsibility and blame, Williams nevertheless rejects any extreme or unqualified skepticism. On the contrary, in Shame and Necessity he advances a vindicatory genealogy — one that unmasks the "illusions" and "fantasies" of our current ethical ideas as they relate to agency and responsibility. Any truthful understanding of free and responsible agency, he argues, must acknowledge the ways in which luck and fate are infused into human ethical life. This is the fundamental ("pessimistic") lesson that we can learn from the ancient Greeks and that Williams seeks to "recover" for us.},
    abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@article{hanpile2025suffered,
    author = {Han-Pile, Béatrice},
    title = {`I Suffered My Deeds More Than I Acted Them': {Hegel} on {Sophocles}' {Oedipus} Plays},
    journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
    year = {2025},
    volume = {46},
    number = {2},
    pages = {213--242},
    doi = {10.1017/hgl.2025.14},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Han-Pile reconstructs Hegel's distinction between Tat and Handlung to explain Oedipus's claim that he "suffered his deeds more than he acted them." Against readings that take Oedipus simply to have mistaken a Tat for a Handlung, she argues the tragic Tat has its own intentional structure, so that Oedipus is rightly held liable (though not ethically responsible) for parricide and incest even though these fall outside what he intended; she traces how Oedipus at Colonus reverses this and engages Williams's reading of the same formula. RELEVANCE: A direct, technically sophisticated re-examination of the action-theoretic machinery (Tat/Handlung) underlying Hegel's account of tragic guilt in the Theban plays, showing both the philosophical resources available to defend a qualified version of Hegel's "necessity of guilt" claim and where it needs revision in light of contemporary responsibility theory (Honoré's outcome responsibility) and Williams's competing interpretation. POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction and partial revision of Hegel's tragic action theory from within contemporary philosophy of action, in explicit dialogue with the Williams-inspired moral luck literature.},
    keywords = {Han-Pile, Hegel, Tat Handlung, Oedipus, moral luck, action theory, High},
    abstract = {I reconfigure Hegel's distinction between Tat (deed) and Handlung (action) to illuminate Oedipus's enigmatic formula: `I suffered my deeds more than I acted them'. Most interpreters hold that Oedipus mistook his Tat for a Handlung and wrongly took responsibility for parricide and incest. I argue against the merely causal reading of Tat presupposed by this view that the tragic Tat also has an intentional structure. On the Restrictive Intentionalism about Action (RIA) which underlies Handlung, what counts as my action is only the realization of a conscious intention, accomplished with reasonable knowledge of the relevant circumstances and foreseeable consequences of realizing my intention. On RIA, Oedipus killed the charioteer and married Iocasta: parricide and incest happened to him. By contrast, on the Inclusive Intentionalism about Action (IIA) which underlies the tragic Tat, what counts as my action is everything I bring about in realizing a conscious intention, regardless of reasonable expectations about knowledge of the circumstances or foreseeable consequences of realizing my intention. On IIA, parricide and incest are part of the `whole compass' of Oedipus's deeds. I argue that Oedipus is right to take responsibility for his deeds and draw on Tony Honoré's conception of `outcome responsibility' to characterize the responsibility at stake as blameless liability. Where Oedipus errs is in taking ethical responsibility for his deeds. I show that in Oedipus at Colonus the older Oedipus reverses his position and holds, somewhat surprisingly, that he is innocent and `did nothing'. I argue that this reversal presupposes an implicit shift from IIA to RIA, and that this shift helps to finally make sense of Oedipus's enigmatic formula: Oedipus suffered his deeds (on RIA) more than he acted them (on IIA). I conclude by widening the perspective beyond ancient Greece and engage with Bernard Williams's interpretation of the same formula.},
    abstract_source = {s2}
}

@article{sourvinouinwood1989assumptions,
    author = {Sourvinou-Inwood, Christiane},
    title = {Assumptions and the Creation of Meaning: Reading {Sophocles'} {Antigone}},
    journal = {The Journal of Hellenic Studies},
    year = {1989},
    volume = {109},
    pages = {134--148},
    doi = {10.2307/632037},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Sourvinou-Inwood argues that modern interpretations of the Antigone — including philosophical ones — routinely import assumptions (about individual conscience, the status of unwritten law, and the relation between household and city) that the play's original fifth-century audience did not share, and that a historically disciplined reading must reconstruct the ancient audience's own religious, legal, and social horizon before assigning meaning to the conflict between Antigone and Creon. RELEVANCE: A methodological objection directly applicable to Hegel's reading, which projects a modern (and idealist) conceptual apparatus of "divine law" and "human law" as fully formed ethical substances onto a text whose own culture, Sourvinou-Inwood insists, worked with different and less symmetrical categories; supplies the review's clearest classicist statement of the historical-accuracy objection to philosophical appropriations of the play. POSITION: Historicist classical philology, skeptical of any reading (Hegelian or otherwise) that treats the play's key terms as transparent vehicles for later philosophical concepts.},
    keywords = {Sourvinou-Inwood, historicism, Antigone, audience assumptions, High},
    abstract = {The notion that texts are not read neutrally, but through perceptual filters shaped by culturally determined assumptions which determine perception and reaction would, I believe, be accepted — in some form — by most literary critics by now. But the extent and radical nature of the cultural determination of reading and their methodological implications are often not fully realized. For they entail that, if we wish to read a text such as the Antigone as closely as possible to the ways in which its contemporary audience did, we must reconstruct in detail their cultural assumptions, by means of which meaning was created, and try to read through perceptual filters created by those assumptions; otherwise we will inevitably read through our own assumptions by default, and as these are very different from those of the Athenians of the late 440s, they will inevitably produce very different meanings from theirs.},
    abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@book{oudemanslardinois1987tragic,
    author = {Oudemans, Th. C. W. and Lardinois, A. P. M. H.},
    title = {Tragic Ambiguity: Anthropology, Philosophy and {Sophocles'} {Antigone}},
    year = {1987},
    publisher = {Brill},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Combining structuralist anthropology with a sustained critical engagement with the philosophical tradition (including Hegel by name), Oudemans and Lardinois argue that the Antigone stages an ambiguity in the very structure of Greek ethical and religious categories that cannot be resolved by any philosophical synthesis; where philosophers from Hegel onward have sought to extract a determinate lesson or dialectical resolution from the play, the authors argue the text itself resists such closure and instead exposes the limits of philosophical "reason" when confronted with archaic religious and kinship categories. RELEVANCE: The most direct book-length classicist confrontation with Hegel's reading found in this search: it explicitly targets the philosophical tradition's tendency (paradigmatically Hegel's) to resolve the play's ambiguity into a higher conceptual unity, and offers a rival, historically and anthropologically grounded account of why that ambiguity is irreducible. POSITION: Anthropologically informed classical philology in critical dialogue with the philosophical (Hegelian and post-Hegelian) reception of the Antigone; a primary source for the historical/philological critique strand of this domain.},
    keywords = {Oudemans, Lardinois, tragic ambiguity, anthropology, Hegel critique, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{segal1981tragedy,
    author = {Segal, Charles},
    title = {Tragedy and Civilization: An Interpretation of {Sophocles}},
    year = {1981},
    publisher = {Harvard University Press},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Segal reads Sophoclean tragedy as dramatizing the fragile, always-threatened boundary between civilization — law, the city, the human construction of meaning — and the forces (natural, bestial, divine, and psychological) that constantly threaten to overwhelm it, with heroic figures like Antigone occupying an ambiguous space at the very edge of the civilized order they simultaneously uphold and violate. RELEVANCE: Segal's civilization/wildness axis offers an independent, historically grounded classicist framework for Antigone's position between household and city that does not map onto Hegel's family/state, divine-law/human-law dichotomy, providing a comparative benchmark for assessing whether Hegel's binary schema captures or distorts the play's actual thematic structure. POSITION: Structuralist-influenced literary-historical criticism of Sophocles, independent of the Hegelian interpretive tradition.},
    keywords = {Segal, tragedy, civilization, heroism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{foley2001female,
    author = {Foley, Helene P.},
    title = {Female Acts in Greek Tragedy},
    year = {2001},
    publisher = {Princeton University Press},
    doi = {10.1515/9781400824731},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Foley examines how Greek tragedy repeatedly stages women acting in the public, political, and ethical domains nominally reserved for men, arguing that these plays do not simply reflect a stable ideology of gender polarity (public male/private female) but actively interrogate, complicate, and sometimes destabilize it, with Antigone as a central case of a woman whose "masculine" civic and religious agency generates the play's core tensions. RELEVANCE: Provides a historically and textually grounded feminist classicist account of Antigone's gendered agency against which both Hegel's own reading (which treats Antigone's ethical standpoint as tied to the family and to an intuitive, unreflective mode of knowing gendered feminine) and the subsequent Hegelian feminist reception (covered elsewhere in this review) can be checked for philological accuracy. POSITION: Feminist classical philology attentive to genre and performance context, offering a scholarly alternative to philosophically driven (including Hegelian) accounts of gender in the play.},
    keywords = {Foley, gender, Greek tragedy, Antigone, feminist classics, Medium},
    abstract = {Although Classical Athenian ideology did not permit women to exercise legal, economic, and social autonomy, the tragedies of Aeschylus, Sophocles, and Euripides often represent them as influential social and moral forces in their own right. Scholars have struggled to explain this seeming contradiction. Helene Foley shows how Greek tragedy uses gender relations to explore specific issues in the development of the social, political, and intellectual life in the polis. She investigates three central and problematic areas in which tragic heroines act independently of men: death ritual and lamentation, marriage, and the making of significant ethical choices. Her anthropological approach, together with her literary analysis, allows for an unusually rich context in which to understand gender relations in ancient Greece. This book examines, for example, the tragic response to legislation regulating family life that may have begun as early as the sixth century. It also draws upon contemporary studies of virtue ethics and upon feminist reconsiderations of the Western ethical tradition. Foley maintains that by viewing public issues through the lens of the family, tragedy asks whether public and private morality can operate on the same terms. Moreover, the plays use women to represent significant moral alternatives. Tragedy thus exploits, reinforces, and questions cultural clichés about women and gender in a fashion that resonates with contemporary Athenian social and political issues.},
    abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@book{griffith1999sophocles,
    author = {Griffith, Mark},
    title = {Sophocles: {Antigone}},
    year = {1999},
    publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Griffith's Cambridge Greek and Latin Classics commentary provides a philologically detailed introduction and line-by-line commentary on the Antigone, situating the play's language, staging, legal and religious background, and reception (including its philosophical afterlife) within the concrete historical conditions of late fifth-century Athens. RELEVANCE: As a standard scholarly commentary, Griffith's edition functions as the philological control against which specific claims Hegel makes about the play's action, characters, and legal-religious background (e.g., the status of the burial decree, the nature of Antigone's and Creon's authority) can be checked for textual and historical accuracy. POSITION: Authoritative classical-philological commentary; a reference point rather than a polemical intervention, but indispensable for assessing the textual accuracy of any philosophical reading of the play.},
    keywords = {Griffith, commentary, Sophocles, philology, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{loraux1987tragic,
    author = {Loraux, Nicole},
    title = {Tragic Ways of Killing a Woman},
    year = {1987},
    publisher = {Harvard University Press},
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Loraux argues that Greek tragedy assigns strikingly different modes of death to men and women — men typically dying by the sword in an assertion of heroic, civic identity, women typically dying by hanging, a death coded as domestic, private, and closer to a rejection of the feminine role than its heroic fulfillment — and reads the deaths of tragic heroines, including Antigone, against this gendered symbolic grid rather than against a general, ungendered concept of heroic or ethical action. RELEVANCE: Loraux's historically specific, gender-differentiated account of tragic death directly complicates any reading (Hegel's included) that treats Antigone's death as the symmetrical, gender-neutral counterpart to a male hero's or as straightforwardly assimilable to a universal category of "ethical substance"; it supplies classicist evidence that the play's gender coding runs deeper and is more asymmetrical than Hegel's schema of complementary but equal ethical powers allows. POSITION: Feminist and anthropologically inflected classical philology, part of the same French tradition (with Vernant and Vidal-Naquet) that reads tragedy as encoding culturally specific, historically located categories rather than universal philosophical oppositions.},
    keywords = {Loraux, gender, death, tragedy, Antigone, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{stevanovic2013private,
  author = {Stevanović, Lada},
  title = {Private is (not) public: About Antigone's mourning voice and its echo in Hegel and Kierkegaard},
  journal = {Filozofija i drustvo},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {24},
  number = {1},
  pages = {254--272},
  doi = {10.2298/fid1301254s},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stevanović rereads Hegel's and Kierkegaard's interpretations of Antigone against the background of research on Sophocles' text and its performance context in fifth-century Athenian theatre, focusing on the political dimension of Antigone's mourning voice and burial action--the subject Hegel recognizes as ethically significant.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a classicist-informed comparison of the two earliest and most consequential philosophical appropriations of Antigone, directly juxtaposing Hegel's and Kierkegaard's readings rather than treating Kierkegaard in isolation, and asking what each obscures about the play's original political-religious context of mourning.

  POSITION: Comparative reception study situating both Hegel and Kierkegaard against classicist scholarship on Athenian funerary practice and gender.
  },
  keywords = {Kierkegaard, mourning, reception-history, High},
  abstract = {This paper presents a rereading of the interpretations of Antigone by Hegel and Kierkegaard on the grounds of research of Sophocles’ text and its performance in Athenian theatre in the context of socio-political climate of the fifth century Athens. Focus is placed on the political aspect of theatre, as well as on the figure of Antigone, her voice and her action, which is the subject recognized by Hegel. However, what this interpretation lacks is the notion that Antigone is political and not pre-political figure. This political aspect reveals itself within the research of ancient Greek lamentation and funeral ritual as an exclusively female practice in ancient Greek tradition, which was subjected to regulations and control in particular by the law of Solo (6th ct. BC). However, new political organization was not based on family relation and aristocratic clans, as before, but exclusively on political bodies. So, for example the vendetta, which was formulated by women during the lamentation, was banned by law. Still, in spite of many laws and regulations by the state, and later on (in the Byzantine period) the church, women in Greece succeeded in keeping their important position in all the practices around the dead, almost until the end of the XX century. So, we see the example of traditional practice that functions on the margins of the society endangering and controlling its official political structure in pre-modern societies. What are the echoes of the political figure of Antigone, as a woman in charge of the family funeral duties, in the text of Hegel and in the text of Kierkegaard. Where is her voice? And does she act politically or privately?},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{jerr2014modern,
  author = {Jerr, Nicole},
  title = {Modern and Tragic?: Kierkegaard's Antigone and the Aesthetics of Isolation},
  journal = {Philosophy and Literature},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {38},
  number = {1},
  pages = {188--203},
  doi = {10.1353/phl.2014.0002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jerr examines Kierkegaard's essay "The Tragic in Ancient Drama Reflected in the Tragic in Modern Drama" (Either/Or I), arguing that Kierkegaard's imagined "modern Antigone"--who inherits reflective knowledge of Oedipus's crime--is defined by an aesthetics of isolation that separates her from the communal, substantial ethical world Hegel locates her within.

  RELEVANCE: Central secondary source for how Kierkegaard's aesthetic sphere reworks Hegel's Antigone: where Hegel's Antigone acts from unreflective ethical certainty grounded in Sittlichkeit, Kierkegaard's modern figure is paralyzed by subjective, hereditary guilt, marking a decisive divergence from Hegelian ethical substance.

  POSITION: Kierkegaard reception scholarship reading the modern Antigone essay as a deliberate counter-figure to Hegelian reconciliation.
  },
  keywords = {Kierkegaard, aesthetics, tragic-guilt, High},
  abstract = {Is it possible to be both modern and tragic? Kierkegaard’s modern Antigone, the figure he creates as an exemplar of the modern tragic, is generally taken to be an endorsement of the tragic as an aesthetic ideal, but this overlooks the cautionary elements in Kierkegaard’s essay, “The Tragic,” in Either/Or . Tracing the limits he imposes, I argue that Kierkegaard is acutely aware that his Antigone, the heroine he puts forward, is trapped precisely by her desire to be a tragic figure, which in turn forces a sacrifice of who she is.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{sestigiani2006danish,
  author = {Sestigiani, Sabina},
  title = {A Danish Antigone: The Legacy of Ancient Greek Consciousness in the Fragmentation of Modern Tragedy},
  journal = {Colloquy},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {11},
  pages = {60--75},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sestigiani traces how Kierkegaard's "Danish" refiguration of Antigone inherits and fragments the ancient Greek consciousness that Hegel treats as the substantial unity of ethical life, arguing that Kierkegaard's modern tragic subject marks the historical fracturing of that Hegelian unity into reflective subjectivity.

  RELEVANCE: Complements Jerr's reading by foregrounding the historical-consciousness dimension of Kierkegaard's essay--the claim that modernity itself, not just individual psychology, has fragmented the conditions for the kind of ethical totality Hegel finds in Sophocles' Antigone.

  POSITION: Kierkegaard reception scholarship emphasizing the historicity of the shift from ancient to modern tragic consciousness.
  },
  keywords = {Kierkegaard, modernity, tragic-consciousness, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{cha2016hegels,
  author = {Cha, Mi-Ran},
  title = {Hegel's and Lacan's Interpretations of Antigone: Ethical Meaning of Tragedy},
  journal = {The Korean Society for the Study of Moral Education},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {28},
  number = {2},
  pages = {1--22},
  doi = {10.17715/jme.2016.8.28.2.1},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cha directly compares Hegel's and Lacan's readings of Antigone as rival accounts of the ethical meaning of tragedy, contrasting Hegel's dialectic of ethical substance and mutual recognition with Lacan's relocation of Antigone's act into the register of desire beyond the pleasure principle.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a systematic, side-by-side comparison of the two interpretive frameworks that most directly bracket this domain, useful for establishing the precise conceptual axis (recognition/Sittlichkeit versus desire/das Ding) along which Lacan's reading departs from Hegel's.

  POSITION: Comparative Hegel-Lacan scholarship on Antigone as competing ethical theories of tragedy.
  },
  keywords = {Lacan, Hegel, ethics-of-tragedy, Medium},
  abstract = {본 연구는 헤겔과 라캉의 「안티고네」 해석을 비교하여 고찰하면서, 「안티고네」에 나타나 있는 비극의 의미에 관한 양자의 논의가 어떻게 연결되고 또한 대비되는가를 드러내는 데에 목적이 있다. 헤겔과 라캉은 크레온과 안티고네 사이의 비극적 갈등을 ‘인간의 법’과 ‘신의 법’이라는 용어로 표현되는 두 가지 윤리 사이의 딜레마에 비추어 해석하고자 한다는 점에서 공통되며, 헤겔은 그 윤리적 딜레마를 ‘국가의 윤리’와 ‘친족의 윤리’ 사이의 갈등으로, 라캉은 ‘선의 윤리’와 ‘욕망의 윤리’ 사이의 대립으로 파악한다. 헤겔의 해석에서 형제간의 본능적 사랑을 따르는 안티고네의 행위는 공동체의 안전에 대한 위협으로 간주되고, 국가의 법 또는 공동체의 이익을 지키고자 하는 크레온에게 정당성이 부여된다. 이와는 대조적으로, 라캉은 크레온이 따르는 선의 윤리를 기존 체제의 현실적 이익에 봉사하는 권력의 윤리로 규정하면서, 선과 악의 이분법을 넘어서서 순수 욕망을 실현하고자 한 안티고네를 그 작품의 유일한 비극적 영웅으로 간주한다. 크레온에 대한 헤겔의 지지는 개인의 내면적 양심이나 인격은 국가의 법으로 대표되는 공동체의 윤리와 분리되어서는 정당성을 가질 수 없다는 그의 철학적 입장에서 비롯된다. 라캉이 안티고네를 통하여 드러내고자 하는 정신분석의 윤리는 국가의 법에 정당성을 부여하는 윤리적 기준 그 자체에 대한 대안적 윤리의 방향을 제시하는 데에 목적을 둔다. 크레온과 안티고네의 윤리적 갈등에 대한 헤겔과 라캉의 해석은 「안티고네」가 제기하는 질문이 자연과 문명, 개인과 국가, 종교와 정치, 인간의 욕망과 신적 법칙 사이의 관계에 관한 해명과 연관되어 있음을 보여주고 있다.This study aims to compare Hegel s interpretation of Antigone with Lacan s from a viewpoint of ethical dimension in the Greek tragedy. They interpret Antigone commonly, in the sense that they regard the tragic conflict between Creon and Antigone as an ethical dilemma between the laws of humans and the laws of gods . While Hegel regards the opposition of Creon and Antigone as a historical collision of ethics of kinship and ethics of state shown in the developmental process of absolute reason of mankind, Lacan insists that Antigone s disobedience to Creon embodies a radical resistance of ethics of desire(or beauty) against ethics of goodness . According to Hegel, Creon s commends for protecting common interests of state are to consider as based on more legitimate ethics than Antigone s one. In contrast with this, Lacan is eager to concentrate on Antigone who is qualified as the only true heroine because she goes beyond the boundary dividing the good and the bad and realizes herself as a beautiful character. Hegel s favoritism toward Creon is to interpret as predetermined by his philosophical position that individual s inner conscience can be justified only in connection with the laws of community. Compared with this, Lacan s praise for Antigone plays a symbolic role in exemplifying the ethics of psychoanalysis as an alternative ethics. These contrasting approaches to Antigone are finally to estimate in relation with the topics such as nature and civilization , individual and society , ‘religion and politics , and human desire and divine laws .},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{moyaert2014ethics,
  author = {Moyaert, Paul},
  title = {No Ethics without Resistance: How Lacan Understands Moral Sensibility},
  journal = {Philosophy Today},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {58},
  number = {3},
  pages = {309--324},
  doi = {10.5840/philtoday20145122},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Starting from Goethe's puzzlement over Antigone's extravagant justification for burying Polyneices, Moyaert argues that Lacan's reference to this passage in Seminar VII clarifies what is at stake in Lacan's dialogue with Aristotle and Kant--namely that genuine ethical sensibility requires a resistance irreducible to rational justification, which Antigone's speech exemplifies.

  RELEVANCE: Clarifies the philosophical stakes of Lacan's specific textual engagement with Antigone's disputed "extravagant" speech (the notorious lines on the irreplaceability of the brother), showing how Lacan uses this crux to distinguish his ethics from both Kantian universalizability and Hegelian mediated recognition.

  POSITION: Lacanian ethics scholarship reading Antigone's resistance to justification as the core of Lacan's departure from rationalist and dialectical ethics alike.
  },
  keywords = {Lacan, ethics, desire, Medium},
  abstract = {In the posthumously published Conversations of Goethe with Eckermann and Soret, Goethe expresses no small degree of perplexity concerning a short passage in the tragedy of Antigone: There is a passage in Antigone which I always look upon as a blemish, and I would give a great deal for an apt philologist to prove that it is interpolated and spurious.1 Goethe's open-ended request is hardly compatible with the popular picture that people form of Anti- gone's motivations, namely that she acted solely out of a sense of duty and love for her brother because he belonged to the inner circle of the family. What then is this passage that so perplexed Goethe? the heroine has, in the course of the piece, explained the noble motives for her action, and displayed the elevated purity of her soul, she at last, when she is led to death, brings forward a motive which is quite unworthy, and almost borders upon the comic. At this point Goethe then paraphrases the Greek text (lines 902-12) in the following way: [Antigone] says that, if she had been a mother, she would not have done, either for her dead children or for her dead husband, what she has done for her brother. For, says she, 'if my husband died I could have had another, and if my children died I could have had others by my new husband. But with my brother, the case is different. I cannot have another brother; for since my mother and father are dead, there is no one to beget one.' After rewording the text in this way, Goethe then reiterates how crudely bare Antigone's declaration is and reiterates his demand: is, at least, the bare sense of the passage, which in my opinion, when placed in the mouth of a heroine going to her death, disturbs the tragic tone, and appears to me very far fetched-to savor too much of calculation. As I said, I should like a philologist to show us that the passage is spurious. In Goethe's estimation, what is so shocking here is that Antigone makes a dialectical calculation in the face of her death. It is by no means a utilitarian calculus, but rather a rational justifica- tion meant to clarify, here and now, what it is that makes Polynices irreplaceable for her. For Goethe, the justification spoils her purity as a tragic character and unintentionally casts her action in a disturbingly comic light.Antigone explains that she would not have made the same sacrifice for a hus- band that she made for her brother. A husband is replaceable-if you lose one, you can always find another one somewhere else. In other words, Antigone's maternal role does not depend on one particular man, for a different husband could give her a child if she were childless in her first marriage, or he could give her a second child if she were to lose the first. Antigone thereby reduces her husband to a pure and simple procreative function-he is like a variable in an equation that could easily be filled in by any old replacement. According to Goethe, Antigone would not even have carried out the same sacrifice for her own child. This is because, just like a husband, a child is not irreplaceable in the same way as Polynices is. Antigone's reasoning here brings to mind the cruel popular wisdom that having a new child is the best remedy for losing a first. But what is it that makes Polynices so unique for her? The motive for her action is not that he is a member of the family with whom she shared a direct blood relationship. It cannot be due to his direct blood tie to Antigone, for she would not have done what she did for just any family member, nor even for any brother. If she had had another living brother, or if her parents had still been alive and were capable of producing another one, she would have let his body rot in the desert. Was Polynices perhaps her favourite brother? No, this couldn't possibly be the case, for her particular affection for him was not in the game: if personal bonds had been her motive, then her behaviour would not have been influenced by the existence of another brother. …},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{sjoholm2004antigone,
  author = {Sjöholm, Cecilia},
  title = {The Antigone Complex: Ethics and the Invention of Feminine Desire},
  year = {2004},
  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  address = {Stanford, CA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sjöholm traces the relation between ethics and desire across the major philosophical texts that use Antigone as a model for feminine desire--Hegel, Heidegger, Irigaray, Lacan, and Butler--arguing that the recurring notion of "feminine desire" is conditioned by a view of women as prone to excess and deficiency in relation to ethical norms, and asks what would follow if psychoanalysis had taken Antigone rather than Oedipus as its founding figure.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most sustained cross-comparison of Hegel's Antigone with the psychoanalytic (chiefly Lacanian) tradition's reworking of her, situating Lacan's desire-based reading within a longer history of philosophical constructions of feminine ethical excess that begins with Hegel; complements but does not duplicate the specifically gender-theoretic Irigaray/Butler material covered elsewhere in this review, since its central axis is the concept of desire rather than kinship or gender performativity per se.

  POSITION: Psychoanalytic-feminist reception scholarship arguing that the "Antigone complex" names a structural condition of feminine desire running from Hegel through Lacan.
  },
  keywords = {Lacan, feminine-desire, psychoanalysis, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{swiffen2010politics,
  author = {Swiffen, Amy},
  title = {Politics of Law and the Lacanian Real},
  journal = {Law and Critique},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {21},
  number = {1},
  pages = {39--51},
  doi = {10.1007/s10978-009-9064-z},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Swiffen examines the debate between Zizek and Butler over Lacan's concept of the real, arguing that their disagreement--played out partly through competing readings of the Antigone tragedy and law/ethics--reflects differing political commitments rather than differing correctness in interpreting Lacan.

  RELEVANCE: Shows how the Hegelian-Lacanian Antigone becomes contested terrain within contemporary political theory, with Zizek's revolutionary/universalist reading of the Lacanian real opposed to Butler's more particularist, kinship-focused reading--useful for tracing how the Hegel-Lacan-Zizek line intersects with (but is distinct from) the Butler material on gender and kinship covered in the feminist-reception domain.

  POSITION: Legal-political theory analyzing the Zizek-Butler dispute over Lacan's Antigone as a proxy for divergent theories of law and the political.
  },
  keywords = {Lacan, Zizek, Butler, political-theory, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{zizek2014sublime,
  author = {Žižek, Slavoj},
  title = {The Most Sublime Hysteric: Hegel with Lacan},
  year = {2014},
  publisher = {Polity},
  address = {Malden, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reading Hegel through Lacan's characterization of him as "the most sublime hysteric," Zizek argues against the standard image of Hegel as a theorist of totalizing abstraction and reconciliation, proposing instead a Hegel of the concrete, of unresolved negativity, and of revolutionary openness--a dialectic driven by the same structure of unanswerable questioning that Lacan finds in hysteria.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational statement of Zizek's Hegelian-Lacanian project (originally published in French in 1988, prior to his English-language breakthrough), against which later disputes over his reading of Antigone (Dews's critique, Robertson's revision) must be situated; establishes the general interpretive strategy--reading Hegelian dialectic as structured by Lacanian desire and lack rather than by teleological reconciliation--that Zizek later applies specifically to the Antigone material.

  POSITION: Founding text of the Ljubljana School's Hegelian-Lacanian synthesis, read by critics as either revitalizing or distorting Hegel's dialectic.
  },
  keywords = {Zizek, Lacan, Hegel, dialectic, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{dews1995tremor,
  author = {Dews, Peter},
  title = {The Tremor of Reflection: Žižek's Lacanian Dialectics},
  journal = {Radical Philosophy},
  year = {1995},
  volume = {72},
  pages = {17--29},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dews mounts a critical examination of Zizek's Lacanian interpretation of Hegel, questioning why a Lacanian framework should be considered the most promising route to unlocking Hegel's "secret" and probing the interpretive costs of insisting on convergence between Hegel and Lacan even where Lacan's own pronouncements resist such convergence.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the earliest sustained philosophical critique of the Zizek-Lacan-Hegel synthesis that underlies Zizek's treatments of Antigone, establishing the terms (fidelity to Hegel's actual dialectical logic versus productive Lacanian appropriation) that later commentators, including Robertson on the Antigone material specifically, continue to negotiate.

  POSITION: Critical assessment of Zizek's Lacanian Hegel from a broadly post-Habermasian/critical-theory standpoint skeptical of the psychoanalytic overlay.
  },
  keywords = {Zizek, Lacan, Hegel, critique, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{robertson2012antigone,
  author = {Robertson, Brian},
  title = {Antigone: Diabolical or Demonic?},
  journal = {International Journal of Žižek Studies},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {6},
  number = {1},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Robertson offers an alternative reading of Lacan's Antigone commentary in Seminar VII against Zizek's frequent characterization of Antigone as a figure of "diabolical" desire, proposing instead an understanding of Antigone's act as "demonic" in a sense that problematizes the moral categories at work in rival schools of psychoanalytic thought.

  RELEVANCE: A direct internal critique of Zizek's specific reading of Antigone from within Lacanian theory, demonstrating that the Hegelian-Lacanian-Zizekian line is not monolithic but contains live disputes over how to categorize Antigone's ethical status (diabolical evil versus demonic excess).

  POSITION: Lacanian scholarship contesting Zizek's characterization of Antigone's desire, proposing a demonic rather than diabolical reading.
  },
  keywords = {Zizek, Lacan, Antigone, desire, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{chanter2016does,
  author = {Chanter, Tina},
  title = {Does Antigone Stand or Fall in Relation to Hegel's Master--Slave Dialectic? A Response to Derrida's Glas},
  journal = {Paragraph},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {39},
  number = {2},
  pages = {202--219},
  doi = {10.3366/para.2016.0195},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chanter interrogates Derrida's focus in Glas on the unintelligibility of familial love for which Hegel makes Antigone representative, arguing that Derrida largely refrains from interrogating the relation between the master-slave dialectic and sexual difference directly, and identifying a silence in Derrida's reading on Hegel's twofold elision of the master-slave dialectic with the husband-wife relation and of Antigone with the figure of the wife.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a critical assessment of Derrida's deconstructive reading of Hegel's Antigone that goes beyond exposition to identify gaps in Derrida's own account--specifically Derrida's silence on how Hegel symbolically "marries off" Antigone--making it essential for evaluating rather than merely reporting the Derridean reception of Hegel's family chapter.

  POSITION: Feminist-deconstructive critique that reads Glas as itself incomplete in its treatment of sexual difference within Hegel's Antigone material.
  },
  keywords = {Derrida, Glas, master-slave, sexual-difference, High},
  abstract = {In Glas, Derrida focuses his attention on a question regarding the family, on the unintelligibility of familial love for which Hegel makes Antigone representative. The account of the emergence of self-consciousness in the family differs in several crucial ways from the standard account of how Hegelian self-consciousness is constituted in the master–slave dialectic. Most notably, the achievement of self-consciousness through familial love involves no risk of life, no struggle to the death, no conflict. While Derrida refrains from interrogating the relation between the master–slave dialectic and sexual difference directly, he interrogates the peaceful recognition that Hegel says occurs between Polynices as brother and Antigone as sister. I explore the silences that punctuate Derrida's discussion of Antigone, especially his silence on Hegel's twofold elision of the master–slave dialectic with the husband–wife relation, and of Antigone with the figure of the wife. By symbolically marrying her off, Hegel subordinates Antigone to a symbolic husband.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kramer2014derridas,
  author = {Kramer, Sina},
  title = {Derrida's ``Antigonanette'': On the Quasi-Transcendental},
  journal = {The Southern Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {52},
  number = {4},
  pages = {521--551},
  doi = {10.1111/sjp.12084},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on both Glas and Derrida's 1971-72 lecture course "La famille de Hegel," Kramer argues that Derrida's concept of the quasi-transcendental is developed precisely through his reading of Hegel's Antigone, coining "Antigonanette" to name the way Antigone functions in Derrida's text as neither fully inside nor outside Hegel's system.

  RELEVANCE: Offers the most technically precise account of how Derrida's general philosophical apparatus (the quasi-transcendental) is worked out through the specific case of Antigone, connecting the abstract deconstructive machinery of Glas to its concrete textual occasion in Hegel's Phenomenology.

  POSITION: Derrida scholarship treating the Antigone material as a paradigm case for the quasi-transcendental structure of deconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {Derrida, Glas, quasi-transcendental, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract In this article, I rely both onDerrida's 1974 workGlas, as well asDerrida's 1971–72 lecture course, “La famille de Hegel,” to argue that the concept of the quasi‐transcendental is central toDerrida's reading ofHegel and to trace its implications beyond theHegelian system. I followDerrida's analysis of the role ofAntigone—or, as the lecture course has it, “Antigonanette”—inHegel's thought to argue that the quasi‐transcendental indicates a re(con)striction of empirical difference into the transcendental, which is thereby only ever provisionally transcendental. I then argue that the economy of difference indicated by the quasi‐transcendental is neither a general economy, nor is it in each case singular, but rather it ambivalently oscillates between these two. Finally, I treat the temporality of the quasi‐transcendental, arguing that the economy of difference indicated by the quasi‐transcendental is not prior to the re(con)striction of empirical difference, but is paradoxically produced by it by being retroactively constituted. I take up this analysis for the sake of describing what I contend is the quasi‐transcendental structure of constitutive exclusion, a way of understanding the conceptual structure of political bodies, and the political structure of concepts.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{charen2011purity,
  author = {Charen, Hannes},
  title = {``The Purity of Her Crime''--Hegel Reading Antigone},
  journal = {Monatshefte},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {103},
  number = {4},
  pages = {504--516},
  doi = {10.1353/mon.2011.0100},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Charen examines how Hegel's reading of Antigone in the Phenomenology of Spirit, organized around war, burial, and incest, first grants Antigone the achievement of full ethical recognition through her grasp of two contradictory laws (divine and communal) but then represses this speculative accomplishment through her fateful disappearance from the text--a structure Derrida identifies and interrogates in Glas.

  RELEVANCE: Works through the specific textual mechanics by which Hegel's Phenomenology both elevates and erases Antigone, providing close-reading support for Derrida's more general deconstructive claim (elaborated in Glas) that Hegel's system depends on exclusions it cannot fully acknowledge.

  POSITION: Close-reading scholarship bridging Hegel's Phenomenology text and Derrida's deconstructive diagnosis of its exclusions.
  },
  keywords = {Derrida, Hegel, Phenomenology, exclusion, Medium},
  abstract = {In Glas Derrida asserts that Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit, in its reading of Antigone, favors consciousness (over the unconscious) by first acknowledging the achievement of ethical plenitude by Antigone, as she comes to full recognition of two contradictory laws, that of the divine and that of the communal spheres, and consequently repressing this speculative accomplishment by her fateful disappearance from both texts. This article complicates the argument by looking at the role that literature takes not only in philosophy, but in the expression of speculative dialectics as such. What for Hegel can be included in a system? How does the impossible figure (Antigone), its expression and then exclusion enter and abandon the system? This text looks at war, burial and incest expressed by the role of Antigone in the Phenomenology of Spirit. (HC)},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{baptist2021derridas,
  author = {Baptist, Gabriella},
  title = {Derrida's Glas between Hegel and Levinas},
  journal = {Levinas Studies},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {15},
  pages = {161--173},
  doi = {10.5840/levinas2022102725},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baptist reads Glas against the background of Derrida's earlier confrontation with Levinas, arguing that Derrida's engagement with Hegel (including the Antigone/family material) does not oppose the archeological totality of Hegel's system with the eschatological infinity of Levinasian metaphysical alterity, but rather with the finite infinity of a transgressive desire for contingency.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Derrida's reading of Hegel's Antigone within Derrida's broader triangulated relationship to both Hegel and Levinas, clarifying that the "remainder" Derrida locates in Hegel's system is not simply a Levinasian ethical alterity but a distinct, more contingency-oriented category--useful for distinguishing Derrida's deconstructive stance from adjacent Levinasian ethical critiques of Hegel.

  POSITION: Derrida-Levinas comparative scholarship clarifying the specific philosophical register of Derrida's critique of Hegel in Glas.
  },
  keywords = {Derrida, Levinas, Glas, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{ponton1991aristote,
  author = {Ponton, Lionel},
  title = {Aristote et Hegel. À propos des Antigones de George Steiner},
  journal = {Laval théologique et philosophique},
  year = {1991},
  volume = {47},
  number = {2},
  pages = {261--267},
  doi = {10.7202/400612ar},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ponton's review-essay engages Steiner's Antigones to assess how Steiner positions Hegel's reading of the play against Aristotle's account of tragedy and the polis, evaluating Steiner's claim that the German idealists (Hegel above all) transformed Antigone into the pivotal test case for modern philosophical consciousness.

  RELEVANCE: Provides scholarly uptake of Steiner's reception-historical argument specifically on the Hegel-Aristotle axis, useful for assessing how Steiner's broad reception history frames Hegel's specific contribution relative to the ancient philosophical tradition on tragedy that the dissertation also engages.

  POSITION: Review-article assessing Steiner's placement of Hegel within the Aristotle-to-idealism genealogy of Antigone interpretation.
  },
  keywords = {Steiner, reception-history, Aristotle, Medium},
  abstract = {L'A. soutient contre George Steiner que dans sa presentation de l'Antigone de Sophocle, aux chapitres XIII et XV de la Rhetorique, Aristote a bien mis en relief le conflit entre le niveau d'etre familial intemporel et la categorie de l'historique. Il soutient aussi que Hegel dans ses Lecons sur l'histoire de la philosophie n'a pas eleve Antigone au-dessus de Socrate, mais s'est plutot applique a decrire celui-ci comme le heros « qui a pour soi le droit absolu de la conscience decidante »},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{steiner1984antigones,
  author = {Steiner, George},
  title = {Antigones},
  year = {1984},
  publisher = {Clarendon Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Steiner's comparative study surveys the reception of Sophocles' Antigone across European philosophy, literature, and drama from the late eighteenth century onward, arguing that the play--and above all its uptake by the German idealists--became the "pivot of consciousness" through which European thought reflected on itself, with Hegel's reading occupying a central, formative position in this history.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational reception-history text for this entire domain: Steiner's claim that Antigone functions as philosophy's self-reflective test case supplies the framing thesis that later commentators (Burke, Ponton, and others across this review) invoke when explaining why so many major post-Hegelian philosophers felt obliged to write their own Antigone.

  POSITION: Comparative literary-philosophical reception history establishing Hegel's centrality within the broader European philosophical afterlife of Antigone.
  },
  keywords = {Steiner, reception-history, German-idealism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

